const BOOKS=[{"id":"7-1","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":1,"genre":"Informational Passage","title":"Earth: A Planet with Liquid Water","paragraphs":["Earth is one of four rocky planets nearest the Sun, yet it has a remarkable feature: oceans of liquid water cover about seventy-one percent of its surface. Water is important to known life, but it cannot explain our planet's habitability by itself. Earth also has temperatures that allow large amounts of surface water to remain liquid, an atmosphere, and chemical ingredients used by living organisms.","The atmosphere is mostly nitrogen and oxygen. It helps moderate conditions and shields the surface from some hazards. Meanwhile, Earth's outer rocky layer is divided into moving plates. Their motion builds mountains, produces earthquakes, and constantly reshapes the land and seafloor.","Earth is not a still object surrounded by space. Its air, oceans, rocks, and living things interact. A change in one system can influence others. When scientists study climate or water, therefore, they often have to consider the entire planet. The same understanding can guide responsible decisions about the environment people share. Earth is habitable for known life because several necessary conditions occur together."],"source":"NASA. Facts About Earth.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["the tides are recurring movements shaped by gravity","water, air, and temperature make Earth distinctive","a strange bedroom test determines royal identity","a girl’s hopeful images contrast with winter hardship"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["a poor girl selling matches","Earth and its interacting natural systems","two young people searching for a mysterious bell","a vain emperor and two dishonest weavers"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["the third planet from the Sun","orbits around the Sun","a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve","the Earth–Moon system"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["readers must explain why Earth supports familiar forms of life","the tree is unhappy with its present life and longs for the future","the Moon appears to change shape even though its solid body remains intact","coastal water levels change in a repeating pattern"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["she hides a tiny pea beneath many mattresses","the seekers move beyond familiar paths instead of trusting easy answers","the article connects surface water, temperature, and atmosphere","he wears imaginary garments after advisers pretend to admire them"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["planetary diversity becomes a tool for learning how worlds form","Earth emerges as a dynamic system rather than a simple rocky ball","after the celebration it is forgotten and its earlier happiness is lost","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the test is based on an exaggerated sensitivity to a pea","the tree barely notices sunshine or birds while imagining later glory","the recognized planets include four inner rocky worlds and four outer giants","liquid water covers roughly seventy-one percent of Earth’s surface"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["habitable environments depend on several conditions working together","compassion requires noticing suffering that others ignore","curiosity may lead beyond convenient explanations","classification helps organize differences without erasing them"]},{"question":"In context, what does “habitable” mean?","choices":["excessive pride in one’s appearance","an imagined or dreamlike sight","control others unfairly through clever influence","suitable for living things"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["appreciating ordinary moments can prevent regret","environmental systems are interconnected","similar origins can produce very different environments","society has a duty to see people who are left outside"]}],"answers":["B","B","A","A","C","B","D","A","D","B"]},{"id":"7-2","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":2,"genre":"Fable","title":"The Lion and the Mouse","paragraphs":["A lion slept beneath a tree after a long day. A mouse ran across his paw. The lion woke and caught the frightened animal. The mouse begged to be released and promised to repay the kindness one day. The lion laughed. How could such a tiny creature ever help a king of beasts? Still, he opened his paw and let the mouse go.","Days later, hunters trapped the lion in a rope net. He pulled with all his strength, but each struggle tightened the knots. Hearing his roar, the mouse hurried to the sound. She remembered the mercy he had shown her. Instead of running away, she climbed the net and began to chew.","One rope broke, then another. At last the opening was large enough for the lion to escape. He thanked the mouse and admitted that he had judged her by her size. The smallest friend, he learned, may offer the greatest help at the right moment."],"source":"Aesop. Aesop’s Fables (V. S. Vernon Jones, trans.). Project Gutenberg, eBook 11339.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["a small creature repays a powerful one","a father teaches the value of labor through a search","a slow competitor wins by refusing to stop","a fox changes his opinion after failing to reach fruit"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["a small mouse and a lion","a farmer and a remarkable goose","a dog carrying a piece of meat","working ants and a singing grasshopper"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a hot courtyard","a woodland","a meadow changing from summer to winter","a bridge over a stream"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["a lion dismisses a mouse as too weak to help","the sons avoid the hard work needed to maintain the vines","the grasshopper prepares no food for the cold season","the shepherd repeatedly calls for help without danger"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["the tortoise walks steadily while the hare naps","the crow drops small stones into the pitcher","the mouse chews a hunter’s net","the wind blows hard but the sun shines gently"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["no one believes him when a wolf truly appears","winter finds the ants prepared and the grasshopper hungry","the trapped lion gains freedom","the well-cultivated soil produces a rich harvest"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the hare falls asleep before completing the race","the tiny teeth can cut the strong ropes","the traveler grips his coat tighter during the storm","each pebble displaces some water"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["gentle influence can succeed where force fails","greed can ruin a reliable good fortune","great strength does not make small kindness worthless","productive effort is the true source of lasting wealth"]},{"question":"In context, what does “mercy” mean?","choices":["an image seen in water or glass","make ready for something that will happen","an excessive desire for more than one needs","kind treatment of someone in your power"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["contentment guards against needless loss","dishonesty harms both the speaker and others","disappointment can tempt people to make excuses","kindness can return from an unexpected source"]}],"answers":["A","A","B","A","C","C","B","C","D","D"]},{"id":"7-3","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":3,"genre":"Fiction","title":"The Elves and the Shoemaker","paragraphs":["An honest shoemaker had become so poor that he possessed enough leather for only one pair of shoes. At night he cut the pieces and placed them on his workbench. He planned to sew them the next morning. When daylight entered the workshop, the leather had become a perfect pair of shoes.","A customer paid well for them, so the shoemaker bought more leather. Again he prepared the pieces at night, and again completed shoes appeared before sunrise. This continued until the family had enough food and work. Curious, the shoemaker and his wife hid behind a curtain one evening.","They saw two small elves working swiftly, stitching and tapping until every shoe was finished. The couple noticed that their helpers wore thin clothing. To express thanks, the wife sewed tiny outfits and the shoemaker made little shoes. The elves discovered the gifts, danced happily, and departed. The shoemaker no longer depended on magic; his improved business and grateful heart remained. The beautiful shoes showed the elves’ careful craftsmanship."],"source":"Grimm, J., & Grimm, W. Grimms’ Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 2591.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2591","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["a rescue succeeds because four talents fit together","a modest cat is prepared when trouble arrives","secret help leads to grateful giving","four unwanted animals find safety as a team"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["a poor shoemaker and two helpful elves","four brothers with different expert skills","a cat and a boastful fox","an elderly grandfather and a small child"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a palace beside a deep pool","a small shoe workshop","a forest path","a castle with a secret underground path"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["the princess loses a golden ball and makes a promise she regrets","a princess is captured and no single brother can rescue her","the servant must complete impossible tasks to win a princess","the shoemaker has leather for only one final pair"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["he cuts the leather and discovers finished shoes in the morning","they cooperate to frighten robbers out of a cottage","he assists animals and later asks for their help","the brothers combine searching, stealing, shooting, and tailoring skills"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the broken spell reveals a prince","the parents recognize their cruelty and change their behavior","the helpers receive clothing and the shoemaker rebuilds his livelihood","his odd procession makes a princess laugh"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the mysterious shoes are sewn with extraordinary skill","each brother contributes a different ability to the rescue","the princess initially refuses to welcome her unlikely helper","ants, ducks, and bees remember earlier kindness"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["gratitude can turn unexpected help into a lasting friendship","helping others creates relationships that can solve problems","specialized talents become powerful when coordinated","one useful skill can be better than many empty claims"]},{"question":"In context, what does “craftsmanship” mean?","choices":["changed by a magical power","skill in making things carefully","work together toward a shared goal","seemingly unable to be done"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["generosity can create a circle of goodwill","gratitude requires action rather than pleasant words","respect for others can matter more than clever appearances","practical readiness matters more than impressive talk"]}],"answers":["C","A","B","D","A","C","A","A","B","A"]},{"id":"7-4","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":4,"genre":"Bible Scripture","title":"The Good Samaritan: A Neighbor in Need","paragraphs":["A traveler was going down a road from Jerusalem to Jericho when robbers attacked him. They took his belongings and left him injured. A priest saw the man but continued on the other side of the road. Later, another religious worker also passed without helping. The wounded traveler remained alone.","Then a Samaritan came along. Instead of ignoring the stranger, he stopped, cleaned and bandaged his wounds, and placed him on his own animal. The Samaritan brought the injured man to an inn and cared for him. Before leaving, he paid the innkeeper to continue the care and promised to cover any additional reasonable expense.","Jesus told this story after someone asked whom people should consider their neighbor. The surprising helper came from a group that many listeners regarded with suspicion. Kindness in the parable was therefore not limited by group membership. The neighbor was the person who showed mercy through action, even when doing so required time, effort, and money."],"source":"Luke 10:25–37; original classroom paraphrase of the Good Samaritan parable (KJV)","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best captures the central teaching of this scripture-based reading?","choices":["Wisdom includes recognizing both meaningful action and human limits","Loyalty can require choosing companionship despite uncertainty","Responsible courage may involve calculated personal risk","Compassion is demonstrated by helping a person in need"]},{"question":"Which person or group is most central to the account?","choices":["the reflective speaker of Ecclesiastes","the Samaritan who helped the traveler","Ruth and her mother-in-law Naomi","David, the young shepherd"]},{"question":"What context or event establishes the situation?","choices":["Mordecai warns Esther about a threat to her people","an army fears Goliath’s repeated challenge","a sequence of contrasting seasons in human life","the road from Jerusalem to Jericho"]},{"question":"Which tension or problem drives the account?","choices":["people cannot command every outcome through effort","the audience does not share every premise of Paul’s belief","a traveler is hurt and abandoned by robbers","soldiers are intimidated by a heavily armed opponent"]},{"question":"Which important action or decision is described?","choices":["the Samaritan stops and treats the wounds","the speaker sets paired experiences beside one another","David selects a sling and smooth stones","Ruth chooses to travel with Naomi"]},{"question":"What result or response follows?","choices":["David defeats the warrior in the confrontation","the two women arrive together in Bethlehem","the innkeeper receives money to continue care","the reflection encourages humility and appreciation"]},{"question":"Which detail gives specific support to the account?","choices":["Naomi releases her daughters-in-law from obligation","two earlier travelers pass without helping","David had defended sheep from dangerous animals","work brings satisfaction but not permanent certainty"]},{"question":"What inference about motive or meaning is most justified?","choices":["describing life’s changes is not endorsing every possible act","David trusts experience and conviction over unfamiliar armor","careful timing can strengthen a difficult appeal","being a neighbor is shown through actions rather than labels"]},{"question":"Which phrase best explains a significant word or idea in the passage?","choices":["deliberate means planned with careful thought","mercy means compassionate help toward someone suffering","intimidated means made fearful or discouraged","rhetorical means concerned with persuasive communication"]},{"question":"Which broader interpretation is most consistent with the passage?","choices":["the parable challenges prejudice about who may act kindly","the apparent opposites create a nuanced rather than simple lesson","the narrative links influence to an obligation toward vulnerable people","the mixed response shows the difference between a strong argument and universal acceptance"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"7-5","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":5,"genre":"Novel","title":"Dorothy and the Yellow Brick Road","paragraphs":["A powerful cyclone lifted Dorothy's house from the Kansas prairie and carried it into a land she had never seen. The world outside her door was bright and astonishing, but beauty did not erase her worry. Dorothy wanted to return to the familiar people who loved her.","People in the new land told her that the Wizard in the Emerald City might know how to help. She put on the magical silver shoes left by the wicked witch in the original novel and began following a road made of yellow bricks. The road offered a visible direction, yet she had no reliable idea how long the journey would take or what dangers might appear.","Her decision is important because a goal does not solve a problem by itself. Dorothy must leave the place where she feels temporarily safe, trust strangers cautiously, and learn along the way. The novel turns the search for home into an exploration of friendship, courage, and the resources people discover within themselves. The Emerald City became Dorothy’s immediate destination, not her final home."],"source":"Baum, L. F. The Wonderful Wizard of Oz. Project Gutenberg, eBook 55.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/55","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["moons vary widely in structure and environment","a hidden garden becomes the center of Mary’s change","Dorothy begins a journey to find her way home","Alice follows a curious clue into an unfamiliar world"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["Earth rotating and orbiting the Sun","Dorothy and the Scarecrow","Dorothy, a girl far from home","Alice, an inquisitive young girl"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a busy coastal community","a meadow and a deep rabbit hole","a peculiar garden tea party","the unfamiliar Land of Oz"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["a cyclone carries Dorothy away from Kansas","a white rabbit behaves in a way that defies ordinary expectations","Mary hears of a garden kept locked for years","Alice’s sudden changes in size create confusion and danger"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["Alice follows it without fully considering what may happen","Alice asks questions and challenges contradictory statements","she tries to understand her surroundings and speak with other creatures","she chooses to travel toward the Emerald City to seek advice"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["her journey begins with a goal and uncertainty about the path","the exchange exposes how words can confuse as well as communicate","readers can explain the calendar’s occasional extra day","she enters a strange world where normal rules seem unreliable"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the Scarecrow can speak thoughtfully despite doubting his intelligence","the yellow brick road offers direction but not a guarantee of safety","Titan has a thick atmosphere and Enceladus has a subsurface ocean","the rabbit checks a pocket watch while hurrying"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["hope becomes practical when combined with action","places can hold memories as well as physical objects","not every confident answer is logically sound","new environments can make familiar behavior ineffective"]},{"question":"In context, what does “destination” mean?","choices":["a natural body orbiting another larger body","a statement that conflicts with another statement","the act of influencing someone’s choice through words or example","the place someone hopes to reach"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["accurate measurement sometimes requires practical adjustment","comparison can correct assumptions created by one example","having a direction is different from knowing every answer","actions can reveal abilities that labels deny"]}],"answers":["C","C","D","A","D","A","B","A","D","C"]},{"id":"7-6","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":6,"genre":"Short Story","title":"The Princess and the Pea","paragraphs":["A young prince traveled through many countries searching for a princess he could marry. Every candidate seemed to disappoint him in some way. One stormy evening, a visitor knocked at the castle gate. Rain had soaked her clothes, and her hair was tangled by the wind. She said she was a princess.","The queen did not announce her doubts. Instead, she placed a small pea on the bed frame, piled many mattresses and soft covers above it, and invited the guest to sleep there. In the morning, the queen asked whether she had rested well. The visitor admitted that something hard beneath the bedding had kept her awake.","The queen treated this discomfort as proof of royal sensitivity. The prince celebrated because his search was over. Yet the peculiar test leaves room for a thoughtful reader to ask questions. Does discomfort truly reveal character? Might a person with a different upbringing also notice the pea? Andersen's miniature tale can be read as a humorous examination of class expectations and the strange ways people decide who belongs. The skeptical queen treated one uncomfortable night as decisive evidence."],"source":"Andersen, H. C. Andersen’s Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1597.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1597","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a girl’s hopeful images contrast with winter hardship","a strange bedroom test determines royal identity","a distant bell invites a search for deeper meaning","comparison reveals both patterns and exceptions among planets"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["a visiting princess and a skeptical queen","the Moon moving around Earth","a vain emperor and two dishonest weavers","the eight planets of the solar system"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a royal guest room","a forest beyond a busy town","a forest and a decorated house","an imperial city during a public parade"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["planets differ greatly despite belonging to the same system","the queen doubts that a rain-soaked visitor is a true princess","the scholar’s shadow gains power and challenges its former owner","the child is cold and has not sold her matches"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the article connects tidal motion to gravitational forces","the shadow dresses as an important man and manipulates social opinion","she hides a tiny pea beneath many mattresses","the explanation follows changing views of its sunlit half"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["moon phases are understood as patterns of illumination","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","after the celebration it is forgotten and its earlier happiness is lost","the guest reports an uncomfortable night and is accepted"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the sound seems different from each crowded street","the tree barely notices sunshine or birds while imagining later glory","the test is based on an exaggerated sensitivity to a pea","liquid water covers roughly seventy-one percent of Earth’s surface"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["curiosity may lead beyond convenient explanations","a test may reveal more about its maker’s beliefs than the person tested","classification helps organize differences without erasing them","habitable environments depend on several conditions working together"]},{"question":"In context, what does “skeptical” mean?","choices":["difficult to explain or understand","not easily convinced without evidence","control others unfairly through clever influence","suitable for living things"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["appearances and standards of status can be questioned","status should not replace truth as a standard for judgment","honesty may require courage when a crowd agrees","similar origins can produce very different environments"]}],"answers":["B","A","A","B","C","D","C","B","B","A"]},{"id":"7-7","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":7,"genre":"Essay","title":"Why a Second Reading Matters","paragraphs":["A first reading tells us what happens. A second reading can show us why it matters. Imagine finishing a fable and remembering only that one animal escaped. When we return to the story, we may notice a small choice that made the escape possible. The second look does not change the printed words; it changes what we are prepared to see.","Rereading is useful because attention is limited. During a first encounter we follow names, events, and unfamiliar words. Once those demands become easier, we have more room to ask questions. Why did the character refuse advice? Why does the author repeat an image? Which detail proves an idea? These questions turn reading into an investigation.","A student need not reread every line of every book. The practical habit is to return to a passage when an answer is uncertain, a claim seems surprising, or an explanation needs evidence. Good readers are not those who never miss anything. They are those who know how to check what they think they understood."],"source":"Original educational essay; reflective reading practice connected to Emerson, The American Scholar, in Essays","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/16643","questions":[{"question":"Which statement is the essay’s primary claim?","choices":["Independent judgment needs evidence and willingness to revise","Social pressure can make obvious errors seem acceptable","A trustworthy inference considers context and alternatives","Rereading helps a reader notice reasons and supporting details"]},{"question":"Which example does the writer use to clarify the claim?","choices":["an object discovered near a crime","the example of a remembered fable","NASA’s DART collision with Dimorphos","the crowd in the emperor’s tale"]},{"question":"Which difficulty or mistaken belief is discussed?","choices":["fear of being judged foolish","the risk of treating one observation as proof","the expectation that results should arrive immediately","the limits of attention during a first reading"]},{"question":"Which contrast is used to develop the argument?","choices":["waiting without action versus purposeful persistence","shared agreement contrasted with independent observation","reading once for events versus twice for interpretation","uncritical acceptance versus automatic rejection"]},{"question":"Which supporting action or reasoning appears in the essay?","choices":["return to a passage when evidence is uncertain","the tortoise keeps moving toward the finish","a spacecraft strikes a moonlet to change orbital motion","a child states what others refuse to acknowledge"]},{"question":"What consequence or outcome does the writer identify?","choices":["a reasoned position remains open to better evidence","steady practice can support long-term improvement","an unsupported guess can become a checked explanation","the result informs but does not settle planetary-defense questions"]},{"question":"Which detail most directly supports the argument?","choices":["a famous institution is not itself a complete proof","students first track unfamiliar words and names","asteroids differ in composition, size, and trajectories","people repeat claims without checking sources"]},{"question":"What inference follows from the writer’s reasoning?","choices":["consensus alone does not establish factual truth","a plausible alternative must be tested rather than ignored","habits can be controlled more directly than outcomes","comprehension improves when readers verify a claim"]},{"question":"Which phrase most accurately explains a key term from the essay?","choices":["kinetic impact means changing motion through a collision","investigation means a careful search for answers and evidence","contagious means spreading from one person to another","scrutiny means careful and critical examination"]},{"question":"Which conclusion best respects the essay’s qualifications?","choices":["rereading is a targeted strategy rather than a sign of failure","intellectual humility and critical independence can coexist","the best interpretation separates narrative excitement from reliable proof","scientific confidence grows when conclusions include explicit limits"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"7-8","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":8,"genre":"News Report","title":"Orion Returns After Artemis I Moon Flight","paragraphs":["December 2022 — NASA's Orion spacecraft returned to Earth after an uncrewed journey around the Moon. The mission, called Artemis I, began on November 16, 2022. Orion spent more than three weeks in space before splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on December 11.","The flight tested the spacecraft and the powerful rocket that carried it away from Earth. Engineers wanted to learn whether the systems could survive travel far beyond low Earth orbit and return safely. No astronauts were aboard; testing without passengers was an important part of the plan.","After the splashdown, recovery teams collected the spacecraft. The flight provided information for later Artemis missions. It did not mean that humans had already landed on the Moon again. Instead, the mission marked a successful test step toward future journeys. Readers of a science report should look for this difference between a completed experiment and a larger goal that remains ahead."],"source":"Original retrospective news report based on NASA Artemis I mission records","sourceUrl":"https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-i/","questions":[{"question":"What event is the main focus of this retrospective science report?","choices":["systematic observation supports asteroid-risk decisions","tide knowledge helps people choose safer times","NASA publicly released Webb’s first full-color science images","NASA completed an uncrewed Moon-flight test with Orion"]},{"question":"Which institution or equipment is central to the event?","choices":["planetary-defense scientists","NASA and its Orion spacecraft","NASA’s OSIRIS-REx spacecraft and sample capsule","coastal residents using tidal information"]},{"question":"Which date or time period is reported?","choices":["July 2022","a busy coastal community","September 24, 2023","November 16 to December 11, 2022"]},{"question":"What was the principal purpose of the work?","choices":["some near-Earth objects could eventually pose an impact hazard","a rising tide can surprise people who ignore local conditions","test systems for travel around the Moon and safe return","scientists need evidence that a spacecraft could alter an asteroid’s movement"]},{"question":"Which action took place?","choices":["Orion traveled beyond the Moon and returned to Earth","the capsule landed in Utah after sample collection","DART deliberately strikes the moonlet Dimorphos in September 2022","residents consult tidal predictions before planning activities"]},{"question":"What reported outcome occurred?","choices":["monitoring helps officials distinguish low risks from meaningful threats","scientists received protected samples for laboratory analysis","the spacecraft splashed down in the Pacific Ocean","better timing reduces avoidable exposure to coastal hazards"]},{"question":"Which detail supplies evidence for the report?","choices":["the rocks had been collected in October 2020","no astronauts were carried on the first test flight","the mission tested deflection rather than responding to a known immediate threat","NASA coordinates planetary-defense observations and warnings"]},{"question":"Why is this event considered significant?","choices":["scientific information is most useful when joined with local judgment","beautiful images also support measurement and research","risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured","the success was a preparation step rather than a new Moon landing"]},{"question":"Which option defines a technical word used in the report?","choices":["a prediction about conditions expected later","uncrewed means traveling without people aboard","contamination means unwanted material entering a sample","infrared refers to light wavelengths beyond visible red"]},{"question":"What limitation or caution should a careful reader keep in mind?","choices":["a careful report distinguishes a test from a finished long-term program","planning requires translating evidence into decisions","credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear","retrieving a sample does not by itself prove the origin of life"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"7-9","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":9,"genre":"Poetry","title":"The Path Beside the Creek","paragraphs":["A narrow creek beside our gate\nMoves gently past the morning light.\nIt cannot hurry to be great;\nIt keeps its silver water bright.","A leaf falls down and floats away.\nA pebble holds its place below.\nI watch the water find a way\nThrough quiet turns I do not know.","The path is short. The world is wide.\nI take one step, then two, then three.\nThe little stream becomes my guide:\nKeep moving on; be glad to see.","When noon arrives, I turn for home,\nBut hear the creek beyond the bend.\nIts steady song reminds me how\nA journey starts and need not end."],"source":"Original classroom poem on nature and steady progress; literary reference: Frost, Selected Poems","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/59824","questions":[{"question":"What is the central idea expressed by the poem?","choices":["Knowledge grows through questioning rather than mere possession of books","Observation can increase questions as well as knowledge","Representations are useful but cannot contain reality completely","Small, steady progress offers a lesson for living"]},{"question":"Which image is central to its meaning?","choices":["a lantern between library shelves","a creek moving beside a gate","rain drawing lines on a window","a telescope receiving faint distant light"]},{"question":"What is the main setting or situation in the poem?","choices":["a map considered against a changing physical world","a coast seen through night and dawn","an observatory at midnight","the path and a flowing stream"]},{"question":"Which change is experienced or observed by the speaker?","choices":["the speaker moves from busyness to reflection","the speaker first wants one simple explanation","a leaf moves while a pebble stays still","the speaker moves from certainty toward inquiry"]},{"question":"Which contrast or comparison shapes the imagery?","choices":["the speaker takes steps beside the water","the moon and local wind are contrasted","rain and quiet become connected images","satellite measurement contrasts with lived experience"]},{"question":"What emotional or intellectual movement does the poem show?","choices":["the morning appears refreshed","measurement opens rather than closes interpretation","the creek continues after the speaker goes home","the speaker accepts a more careful explanation"]},{"question":"Which textual detail most clearly supports an interpretation?","choices":["waves and tide provide evidence of ongoing change","the water keeps finding a way through bends","the road seems newly colored after the storm","the speaker writes a figure and also a question"]},{"question":"What might the central image or situation suggest?","choices":["a text is useful when the reader examines it independently","distant astronomy reveals limits of immediate perception","the changing weather parallels a change in attention","the stream suggests patient persistence"]},{"question":"Which option best explains one important word or expression?","choices":["inquiry means purposeful questioning or investigation","steady means continuing without sudden stops","convention means an agreed method of representing something","sufficient means enough to explain or satisfy a need"]},{"question":"How should the closing lines be understood?","choices":["the ending suggests that learning can continue beyond one walk","the final stanza favors evidence over false certainty","the final color image suggests changed perception rather than magic","the ending values careful thinking more than collecting facts"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"7-10","grade":7,"color":"Emerald","num":10,"genre":"Science & Nonfiction","title":"What Makes an Earth Year?","paragraphs":["Every day, Earth turns around its axis. This rotation explains the familiar movement from daylight to darkness and back again. A complete turn takes roughly twenty-four hours. At the same time, Earth travels along a much larger path around the Sun. One orbit, or revolution, takes approximately 365.25 days.","The difference between a day and a year is easy to name, yet the numbers create a practical challenge. A calendar with exactly 365 days would slowly fall behind the planet's orbit, because each real year lasts about one quarter of a day longer. To reduce this mismatch, calendars usually add a leap day every four years, with further rules to maintain accuracy.","These arrangements show that calendars are not the universe itself. They are human systems designed to represent natural patterns. Scientific measurement allows people to improve those systems, while everyday language makes the vast movement of a planet feel ordinary."],"source":"NASA. Facts About Earth.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["Alice struggles with the consequences of her changing size","the calendar reflects Earth’s two different motions","moons vary widely in structure and environment","Tom turns an unwanted chore into an object of desire"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["Alice and the Mad Hatter’s tea-table companions","Earth rotating and orbiting the Sun","natural satellites orbiting planets and other bodies","Dorothy and the Scarecrow"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a hallway flooded with Alice’s tears","different regions of the solar system","a meadow and a deep rabbit hole","Earth’s path through space"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["a white rabbit behaves in a way that defies ordinary expectations","day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names","Tom must paint a fence while other children are free to play","a rising tide can surprise people who ignore local conditions"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["he makes the work appear like a special privilege","she chooses to travel toward the Emerald City to seek advice","the essay compares rotation with revolution","Alice follows it without fully considering what may happen"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["readers can explain the calendar’s occasional extra day","her journey begins with a goal and uncertainty about the path","better timing reduces avoidable exposure to coastal hazards","he joins the journey and begins showing practical judgment"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the garden was closed after a painful family loss","Earth takes about 365.25 days to complete one orbit","tears shed while she was enormous later form a pool","the yellow brick road offers direction but not a guarantee of safety"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["human calendars are models adjusted to natural cycles","new environments can make familiar behavior ineffective","not every confident answer is logically sound","curiosity can lead to discovery but also uncertainty"]},{"question":"In context, what does “revolution” mean?","choices":["one complete trip around another body","a statement that conflicts with another statement","kept apart from other people or places","the place someone hopes to reach"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["accurate measurement sometimes requires practical adjustment","wonder can disturb our assumptions about ordinary life","adaptability begins with recognizing changed circumstances","planning requires translating evidence into decisions"]}],"answers":["B","B","D","B","C","A","B","A","A","A"]},{"id":"8-1","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":1,"genre":"Informational Passage","title":"Eight Planets, Different Worlds","paragraphs":["Our solar system contains eight recognized planets moving around the Sun. The four nearest planets—Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars—are relatively small and rocky. Farther away lie Jupiter and Saturn, which are gas giants, and Uranus and Neptune, commonly described as ice giants. Each follows an orbit, but their sizes, temperatures, atmospheres, and surfaces vary widely.","Earth offers familiar oceans and life. Venus has a remarkably hot surface beneath a thick atmosphere. Mars has a cold, dusty landscape. Jupiter possesses immense storms; Saturn is famous for its rings. The distant ice giants introduce still more unfamiliar conditions.","Grouping planets is helpful because it gives astronomers a starting point for comparison. Yet categories are not complete descriptions. Two rocky planets may have very different climates, just as two giant planets differ in composition. Scientists ask how distance, formation, and later changes helped create these contrasts. Studying our planetary neighborhood also prepares us to understand worlds orbiting distant stars. Astronomers classify planets to organize their observations, not to erase their differences."],"source":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA). About the Planets.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["comparison reveals both patterns and exceptions among planets","a ruler’s pride allows a deception to spread","the tides are recurring movements shaped by gravity","a girl’s hopeful images contrast with winter hardship"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["the eight planets of the solar system","the Moon moving around Earth","a visiting princess and a skeptical queen","a young fir tree"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve","orbits around the Sun","the third planet from the Sun","the Earth–Moon system"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["planets differ greatly despite belonging to the same system","the scholar’s shadow gains power and challenges its former owner","the tree is unhappy with its present life and longs for the future","people hear a distant sound but cannot agree where it comes from"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["the article connects tidal motion to gravitational forces","the article compares rocky inner planets with outer giants","the shadow dresses as an important man and manipulates social opinion","it waits eagerly to grow tall and become a Christmas tree"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","moon phases are understood as patterns of illumination","a child publicly says the emperor is wearing nothing","planetary diversity becomes a tool for learning how worlds form"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the recognized planets include four inner rocky worlds and four outer giants","liquid water covers roughly seventy-one percent of Earth’s surface","the Moon and Sun both exert gravitational pulls related to tides","the weavers claim foolish people cannot see their fabric"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["a test may reveal more about its maker’s beliefs than the person tested","classification helps organize differences without erasing them","constant longing may hide the value of the present","social pressure can make many people repeat a lie"]},{"question":"In context, what does “classify” mean?","choices":["control others unfairly through clever influence","sort things into groups using shared features","difficult to explain or understand","the feeling of expecting something to happen"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["similar origins can produce very different environments","understanding may demand patient exploration","environmental systems are interconnected","knowing natural patterns improves safety and planning"]}],"answers":["A","A","B","A","B","D","A","B","B","A"]},{"id":"8-2","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":2,"genre":"Fable","title":"The Tortoise and the Hare","paragraphs":["The hare loved to boast that no animal could run faster than he could. When he teased a tortoise for walking slowly, the tortoise calmly invited him to a race. The other animals marked a route across the fields. Everyone expected the hare to win.","At the signal, the hare raced ahead until the tortoise looked like a dot behind him. Feeling certain of victory, he rested in the shade and soon fell asleep. The tortoise did not stop to argue or admire the view. Step after step, she moved along the path at her own pace.","When the hare woke, the afternoon was nearly over. He rushed toward the finish, but the tortoise had already crossed it. The crowd cheered the unexpected winner. The tortoise had not become faster than the hare. She had simply used her time wisely, while her rival wasted his advantage."],"source":"Aesop. Aesop’s Fables (V. S. Vernon Jones, trans.). Project Gutenberg, eBook 11339.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a fox changes his opinion after failing to reach fruit","a slow competitor wins by refusing to stop","the sun wins a contest by giving warmth","a father teaches the value of labor through a search"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["the North Wind and the Sun","a farmer and a remarkable goose","a patient tortoise and a speedy hare","a dog carrying a piece of meat"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a small farm","a hot courtyard","a bridge over a stream","a path through the fields"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["grapes hang beyond the fox’s reach","water lies too low inside a narrow pitcher","the hare is proud and underestimates a slow rival","the grasshopper prepares no food for the cold season"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the crow drops small stones into the pitcher","the dog opens its mouth to seize the imagined second piece","the villagers run to him until they stop trusting his calls","the tortoise walks steadily while the hare naps"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["no one believes him when a wolf truly appears","the water rises to the bird’s beak","the tortoise reaches the finish first","the trapped lion gains freedom"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["each pebble displaces some water","his earlier false alarms damage his credibility","the hare falls asleep before completing the race","the tiny teeth can cut the strong ropes"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["steady effort can overcome careless talent","great strength does not make small kindness worthless","people may criticize what they cannot obtain","a truthful warning depends on earned trust"]},{"question":"In context, what does “boast” mean?","choices":["speak too proudly about an ability","sharp or unpleasant in taste","an image seen in water or glass","kind treatment of someone in your power"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["pleasure and responsibility need balance","kindness can be more effective than pressure","patience can protect long-term rewards","persistence matters more than showing off"]}],"answers":["B","C","D","C","D","C","C","A","A","D"]},{"id":"8-3","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":3,"genre":"Fiction","title":"The Golden Goose","paragraphs":["Three brothers went into the forest to gather wood. The older two believed they were wiser than their youngest brother, whom they called Simpleton. When a hungry old man asked for food, they refused to share. Simpleton, however, offered his plain meal without hesitation.","The stranger rewarded him by showing him a tree that contained a goose with golden feathers. Simpleton carried the strange bird to an inn. Curious people tried to pull away its feathers, but each person who touched the goose became stuck. Soon a ridiculous line of people followed Simpleton along the road.","In a nearby castle, a princess had grown so serious that no one could make her smile. When she saw Simpleton and the noisy procession, she burst into laughter. The scene changed the young man's fortune. The tale contrasts the brothers' arrogance with a simple act of generosity. The kindest person receives the most surprising opportunity. The brothers’ ridicule had not prevented Simpleton from acting generously."],"source":"Grimm, J., & Grimm, W. Grimms’ Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 2591.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2591","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a modest cat is prepared when trouble arrives","a traveler solves the mystery of worn-out shoes","a servant succeeds with aid from creatures he spared","a generous traveler wins help through humility"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["a princess and an enchanted frog","a poor shoemaker and two helpful elves","twelve princesses and a watchful traveler","a kind younger brother called Simpleton"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a castle with a secret underground path","a kingdom threatened by a dragon","a forest and a royal town","a forest path"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["others ridicule Simpleton and treat strangers badly","the family treats an aging grandfather without dignity","the aging animals fear they are no longer wanted","the shoemaker has leather for only one final pair"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the youngest prince protects creatures who later assist him","the cat climbs a tree when hunting dogs arrive","he shares food with an old man and receives a golden goose","the child copies the adults by preparing a wooden bowl for their future"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the helpers receive clothing and the shoemaker rebuilds his livelihood","he reveals the secret midnight dances","the animals help recover a ring and gather scattered grain","his odd procession makes a princess laugh"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the child explains that his parents may also grow old","each brother contributes a different ability to the rescue","the princess initially refuses to welcome her unlikely helper","anyone trying to grab the goose becomes stuck to it"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["one useful skill can be better than many empty claims","helping others creates relationships that can solve problems","kindness may open doors that pride keeps closed","promises reveal character when keeping them is inconvenient"]},{"question":"In context, what does “ridicule” mean?","choices":["the respect owed to every person","unkind laughter at another person","trained for one particular kind of work","unable to be seen"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["respect for others can matter more than clever appearances","evidence is more useful than a convenient guess","compassion can be a form of wisdom","gratitude requires action rather than pleasant words"]}],"answers":["D","D","C","A","C","D","D","C","B","A"]},{"id":"8-4","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":4,"genre":"Bible Scripture","title":"Ruth and Naomi: Loyalty on a Difficult Road","paragraphs":["Naomi had lived away from Bethlehem with her family, but tragedy changed her circumstances. Her husband died, and in time both her sons also died. With few resources left, she decided to return to her homeland. She told her daughters-in-law that they were free to remain among their own people and build new lives.","One woman returned home. Ruth, however, refused to leave Naomi alone. Ruth chose to accompany her mother-in-law to Bethlehem, even though doing so meant entering an unfamiliar community and facing uncertainty. Naomi could not promise Ruth wealth or security. The decision was an act of loyalty, not a bargain.","When the two women reached Bethlehem, their arrival raised questions among people who remembered Naomi. They had returned with loss, yet also with the possibility of rebuilding a life together. The account does not claim that loyalty erases hardship. Rather, it presents faithful companionship as one response to grief. Ruth's commitment matters precisely because her future remained uncertain."],"source":"Ruth 1:1–22; original classroom paraphrase (KJV)","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best captures the central teaching of this scripture-based reading?","choices":["Loyalty can require choosing companionship despite uncertainty","Courage can draw on practiced skill rather than appearance","Effective argument may begin with shared evidence and then challenge assumptions","Wisdom includes recognizing both meaningful action and human limits"]},{"question":"Which person or group is most central to the account?","choices":["the Samaritan who helped the traveler","David, the young shepherd","Ruth and her mother-in-law Naomi","the reflective speaker of Ecclesiastes"]},{"question":"What context or event establishes the situation?","choices":["Naomi prepares to return to Bethlehem","the road from Jerusalem to Jericho","a city filled with public debate and religious images","Mordecai warns Esther about a threat to her people"]},{"question":"Which tension or problem drives the account?","choices":["a traveler is hurt and abandoned by robbers","people cannot command every outcome through effort","the audience does not share every premise of Paul’s belief","Naomi has lost her husband and sons"]},{"question":"Which important action or decision is described?","choices":["David selects a sling and smooth stones","Ruth chooses to travel with Naomi","the speaker sets paired experiences beside one another","Esther prepares and arranges a meal to reveal the plot"]},{"question":"What result or response follows?","choices":["listeners react with mockery, curiosity, or belief","the reflection encourages humility and appreciation","the threatened community gains protection","the two women arrive together in Bethlehem"]},{"question":"Which detail gives specific support to the account?","choices":["two earlier travelers pass without helping","Esther asks her community to fast before she acts","Naomi releases her daughters-in-law from obligation","an existing inscription becomes a point of contact"]},{"question":"What inference about motive or meaning is most justified?","choices":["Ruth values belonging and faithfulness more than a promised reward","careful timing can strengthen a difficult appeal","David trusts experience and conviction over unfamiliar armor","persuasion requires attention to an audience without promising agreement"]},{"question":"Which phrase best explains a significant word or idea in the passage?","choices":["pessimistic means emphasizing discouraging aspects or limits","intimidated means made fearful or discouraged","commitment means a lasting promise shown in conduct","rhetorical means concerned with persuasive communication"]},{"question":"Which broader interpretation is most consistent with the passage?","choices":["the mixed response shows the difference between a strong argument and universal acceptance","the story respects grief while showing the strength of solidarity","the parable challenges prejudice about who may act kindly","the narrative links influence to an obligation toward vulnerable people"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"8-5","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":5,"genre":"Novel","title":"Down the Rabbit Hole","paragraphs":["Alice was sitting with her sister on a warm afternoon when she noticed a white rabbit hurrying across the grass. A rabbit alone would hardly have surprised her. This one, however, spoke of being late and pulled a watch from its pocket. Such a strange combination of ordinary animal and human habit immediately captured her attention.","Alice ran after the rabbit and watched it disappear into a hole beneath a hedge. Without stopping to measure the danger, she followed. She found herself falling through a deep shaft. Shelves and cupboards lined its sides, and she had time to wonder how far she might travel before reaching the bottom.","Eventually Alice landed safely and entered a corridor of locked doors. The rabbit was gone. Her curiosity had carried her from a familiar afternoon into a place governed by unfamiliar rules. The episode invites readers to consider the double nature of exploration: the same impulse that leads to discovery can also take us beyond what we understand. Alice’s inquisitive nature pushed her toward a discovery she could not predict."],"source":"Carroll, L. Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland. Project Gutenberg, eBook 11.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["tide knowledge helps people choose safer times","Alice follows a curious clue into an unfamiliar world","the Scarecrow searches for a quality he already displays","a hidden garden becomes the center of Mary’s change"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["coastal residents using tidal information","Earth rotating and orbiting the Sun","natural satellites orbiting planets and other bodies","Alice, an inquisitive young girl"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a meadow and a deep rabbit hole","a large Yorkshire manor and its grounds","Earth’s path through space","a wooden fence outside Aunt Polly’s home"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["a white rabbit behaves in a way that defies ordinary expectations","conversation does not follow the reasonable rules Alice expects","a cyclone carries Dorothy away from Kansas","day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["Alice follows it without fully considering what may happen","he makes the work appear like a special privilege","she listens, observes, and searches for a hidden key","Dorothy invites him to travel to the Wizard"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["her journey begins with a goal and uncertainty about the path","she enters a strange world where normal rules seem unreliable","the exchange exposes how words can confuse as well as communicate","other boys trade small treasures for a chance to paint"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the rabbit checks a pocket watch while hurrying","the table companions argue about riddles and time","the garden was closed after a painful family loss","Earth takes about 365.25 days to complete one orbit"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["the perceived value of an activity can depend on how it is presented","curiosity can lead to discovery but also uncertainty","new environments can make familiar behavior ineffective","scientific categories contain meaningful internal differences"]},{"question":"In context, what does “inquisitive” mean?","choices":["eager to ask questions and investigate","a prediction about conditions expected later","a statement that conflicts with another statement","the act of influencing someone’s choice through words or example"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["critical reading includes examining how language is used","comparison can correct assumptions created by one example","planning requires translating evidence into decisions","wonder can disturb our assumptions about ordinary life"]}],"answers":["B","D","A","A","A","B","A","B","A","D"]},{"id":"8-6","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":6,"genre":"Short Story","title":"The Emperor’s New Clothes","paragraphs":["An emperor loved fine clothing more than the ordinary duties of government. Hearing about this weakness, two strangers announced that they could weave a fabric visible only to wise and capable people. They demanded expensive silk and gold, then pretended to work at empty looms. The ministers who inspected the fabric saw nothing. Afraid of appearing foolish, each praised colors and patterns that were not there.","The emperor visited the workshop and faced the same difficulty. Although he could see only empty space, he admired the supposed cloth. On the day of a grand procession, the weavers pretended to dress him in their marvelous garments. Crowds praised the outfit because nobody wanted to be the first person to disagree.","Then a child spoke aloud: the emperor was wearing no clothes. Others began to whisper the same truth. The emperor sensed that the child was right, but continued the parade. The tale uses humor to examine vanity, dishonesty, and the pressure to accept an opinion simply because many people appear to share it."],"source":"Andersen, H. C. Andersen’s Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1597.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1597","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["a tree learns too late to value its life in the forest","a distant bell invites a search for deeper meaning","the tides are recurring movements shaped by gravity","a ruler’s pride allows a deception to spread"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["the ocean, Moon, and Sun","the Moon moving around Earth","a scholar and his independent shadow","a vain emperor and two dishonest weavers"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a royal guest room","an imperial city during a public parade","a forest and a decorated house","orbits around the Sun"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["the child is cold and has not sold her matches","the Moon appears to change shape even though its solid body remains intact","the tree is unhappy with its present life and longs for the future","the emperor fears admitting that he sees no magnificent cloth"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["he wears imaginary garments after advisers pretend to admire them","the article connects surface water, temperature, and atmosphere","the seekers move beyond familiar paths instead of trusting easy answers","she hides a tiny pea beneath many mattresses"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["planetary diversity becomes a tool for learning how worlds form","the tale ends tragically, exposing neglect of the poor","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","a child publicly says the emperor is wearing nothing"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["liquid water covers roughly seventy-one percent of Earth’s surface","the weavers claim foolish people cannot see their fabric","the tree barely notices sunshine or birds while imagining later glory","the test is based on an exaggerated sensitivity to a pea"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["classification helps organize differences without erasing them","predictable natural cycles can still affect human choices","social pressure can make many people repeat a lie","compassion requires noticing suffering that others ignore"]},{"question":"In context, what does “vanity” mean?","choices":["suitable for living things","excessive pride in one’s appearance","the height difference between high and low tide","an imagined or dreamlike sight"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["similar origins can produce very different environments","knowing natural patterns improves safety and planning","society has a duty to see people who are left outside","honesty may require courage when a crowd agrees"]}],"answers":["D","D","B","D","A","D","B","C","B","D"]},{"id":"8-7","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":7,"genre":"Essay","title":"Patience Is More Than Waiting","paragraphs":["People often describe patience as doing nothing while time passes. The idea is incomplete. A gardener who prepares soil and waters a seed waits, but also performs regular work. A student learning difficult vocabulary may need weeks of practice before new words feel familiar. Neither person can force immediate results, and neither person is inactive.","Aesop's account of the steady tortoise provides a memorable image. What makes the tortoise's progress meaningful is not slowness by itself but continued movement. If the animal had simply remained at the starting line, waiting would have accomplished nothing. The fable separates effort from display: a noisy beginning is less important than sustained action.","Patience therefore combines restraint with purpose. We can control our habits more easily than we can control the speed of every outcome. The distinction matters in classrooms, laboratories, and friendships. To be patient is not always to endure without questioning. Sometimes patience means revising a method, asking for help, and trying again without pretending that improvement must happen instantly."],"source":"Original interpretive essay responding to Aesop, The Tortoise and the Hare","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which statement is the essay’s primary claim?","choices":["Patience involves persistent work rather than passive delay","Social pressure can make obvious errors seem acceptable","An experiment should distinguish a measured result from a universal claim","Independent judgment needs evidence and willingness to revise"]},{"question":"Which example does the writer use to clarify the claim?","choices":["NASA’s DART collision with Dimorphos","a student evaluating a climate report","the gardener and student examples","the example of a remembered fable"]},{"question":"Which difficulty or mistaken belief is discussed?","choices":["the expectation that results should arrive immediately","the risk of treating one observation as proof","the limits of attention during a first reading","confusing disagreement with independent reasoning"]},{"question":"Which contrast is used to develop the argument?","choices":["reading once for events versus twice for interpretation","objectives and observations contrasted with interpretation","dramatic guessing contrasted with evidence-based reasoning","waiting without action versus purposeful persistence"]},{"question":"Which supporting action or reasoning appears in the essay?","choices":["a child states what others refuse to acknowledge","the tortoise keeps moving toward the finish","return to a passage when evidence is uncertain","the investigator compares observations with new facts"]},{"question":"What consequence or outcome does the writer identify?","choices":["an unsupported guess can become a checked explanation","a reasoned position remains open to better evidence","the result informs but does not settle planetary-defense questions","steady practice can support long-term improvement"]},{"question":"Which detail most directly supports the argument?","choices":["fictional detectives encounter evidence arranged by an author","people repeat claims without checking sources","the tortoise would not succeed by staying at the start","asteroids differ in composition, size, and trajectories"]},{"question":"What inference follows from the writer’s reasoning?","choices":["habits can be controlled more directly than outcomes","one successful demonstration does not guarantee all future outcomes","a plausible alternative must be tested rather than ignored","stronger claims ordinarily demand stronger supporting evidence"]},{"question":"Which phrase most accurately explains a key term from the essay?","choices":["scrutiny means careful and critical examination","autonomy means self-directed responsible judgment","restraint means controlling an impulse or reaction","contagious means spreading from one person to another"]},{"question":"Which conclusion best respects the essay’s qualifications?","choices":["the best interpretation separates narrative excitement from reliable proof","the writer argues for effort combined with reasonable expectations","questioning a claim respectfully may protect a community from error","rereading is a targeted strategy rather than a sign of failure"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"8-8","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":8,"genre":"News Report","title":"Webb Telescope Reveals Its First Science Images","paragraphs":["July 2022 — NASA released the James Webb Space Telescope's first collection of full-color science images. The pictures showed distant galaxies and other objects in remarkable detail. They were not taken with an ordinary phone camera: Webb collects light, including infrared wavelengths, that can help scientists study regions and objects difficult to observe in visible light.","One of the images focused on a galaxy cluster called SMACS 0723. Its field of view covered only a tiny patch of sky, yet the image contained many galaxies. The release demonstrated what Webb's instruments could do after careful preparation and testing in space.","The scientific value of the photographs extends beyond their beauty. Astronomers use images together with measurements to ask how galaxies formed and changed. The first-image release was a beginning, not the final answer to every cosmic mystery. Science reporting can celebrate discovery while reminding readers that interpretation requires continued research."],"source":"Original retrospective news report based on NASA, Webb’s First Images","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/webbs-first-images/","questions":[{"question":"What event is the main focus of this retrospective science report?","choices":["NASA publicly released Webb’s first full-color science images","a NASA mission returned material from asteroid Bennu","systematic observation supports asteroid-risk decisions","an intentional spacecraft impact tests a defense idea"]},{"question":"Which institution or equipment is central to the event?","choices":["planetary-defense scientists","NASA and its Orion spacecraft","the James Webb Space Telescope and NASA","coastal residents using tidal information"]},{"question":"Which date or time period is reported?","choices":["July 2022","observatories on Earth and in space","September 24, 2023","space near the asteroid system Didymos"]},{"question":"What was the principal purpose of the work?","choices":["some near-Earth objects could eventually pose an impact hazard","scientists need evidence that a spacecraft could alter an asteroid’s movement","a rising tide can surprise people who ignore local conditions","show the telescope’s observing capabilities"]},{"question":"Which action took place?","choices":["the capsule landed in Utah after sample collection","Webb collected infrared-related observations","scientists locate, track, and refine the predicted paths of objects","residents consult tidal predictions before planning activities"]},{"question":"What reported outcome occurred?","choices":["scientists received protected samples for laboratory analysis","the spacecraft splashed down in the Pacific Ocean","measurements show that the impact changed the moonlet’s orbit","images displayed numerous distant galaxies"]},{"question":"Which detail supplies evidence for the report?","choices":["no astronauts were carried on the first test flight","NASA coordinates planetary-defense observations and warnings","SMACS 0723 occupied a very small patch of sky","the mission tested deflection rather than responding to a known immediate threat"]},{"question":"Why is this event considered significant?","choices":["beautiful images also support measurement and research","risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured","scientific information is most useful when joined with local judgment","samples may preserve clues about the early solar system"]},{"question":"Which option defines a technical word used in the report?","choices":["a prediction about conditions expected later","contamination means unwanted material entering a sample","infrared refers to light wavelengths beyond visible red","uncrewed means traveling without people aboard"]},{"question":"What limitation or caution should a careful reader keep in mind?","choices":["credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear","the first images opened new questions rather than concluding astronomy","a careful report distinguishes a test from a finished long-term program","preparation is more reliable when methods are tested beforehand"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"8-9","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":9,"genre":"Poetry","title":"Rain on the Window","paragraphs":["At dusk the rain unbuttons clouds\nAnd draws its lines upon the glass.\nThe crowded street grows still beneath\nThe silver feet of showers passing.","I thought the day had left no room\nFor one more thought or gentle sound;\nYet every drop repeats a beat\nThat makes a quieter place around me.","The lamp reflects a second room\nWhere shadow walks beside the chair.\nOutside, the branches bow and rise;\nInside, I learn to let things settle.","When morning clears the windowpane,\nThe road wears colors washed anew.\nThe storm was not a final word;\nIt changed the way I looked at blue."],"source":"Original classroom poem on imagery and renewal; literary reference: Frost, Selected Poems","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/59824","questions":[{"question":"What is the central idea expressed by the poem?","choices":["A storm can change how a person perceives ordinary life","Complex events often have multiple contributing causes","Representations are useful but cannot contain reality completely","Observation can increase questions as well as knowledge"]},{"question":"Which image is central to its meaning?","choices":["an atlas with boundaries and symbols","a telescope receiving faint distant light","rain drawing lines on a window","a changing sea and its shoreline"]},{"question":"What is the main setting or situation in the poem?","choices":["a dim room looking toward a street at dusk","a coast seen through night and dawn","an observatory at midnight","a library during an evening reading session"]},{"question":"Which change is experienced or observed by the speaker?","choices":["the reader begins to check claims and dates","the speaker abandons the hope that maps eliminate uncertainty","the speaker first wants one simple explanation","the speaker moves from busyness to reflection"]},{"question":"Which contrast or comparison shapes the imagery?","choices":["the moon and local wind are contrasted","rain and quiet become connected images","the speaker takes steps beside the water","light represents partial guidance rather than complete knowledge"]},{"question":"What emotional or intellectual movement does the poem show?","choices":["the reader gains a more qualified trust in models","measurement opens rather than closes interpretation","the speaker accepts a more careful explanation","the morning appears refreshed"]},{"question":"Which textual detail most clearly supports an interpretation?","choices":["boundaries look fixed although land and rivers change","the water keeps finding a way through bends","the road seems newly colored after the storm","the speaker writes a figure and also a question"]},{"question":"What might the central image or situation suggest?","choices":["the changing weather parallels a change in attention","the ocean symbolizes the limits of simple answers","a text is useful when the reader examines it independently","distant astronomy reveals limits of immediate perception"]},{"question":"Which option best explains one important word or expression?","choices":["convention means an agreed method of representing something","sufficient means enough to explain or satisfy a need","renewal means a sense of freshness or beginning again","inquiry means purposeful questioning or investigation"]},{"question":"How should the closing lines be understood?","choices":["the final stanza treats uncertainty as intellectual opportunity","the final color image suggests changed perception rather than magic","the final stanza favors evidence over false certainty","the poem supports precise maps while questioning claims of total certainty"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"8-10","grade":8,"color":"Sapphire","num":10,"genre":"Science & Nonfiction","title":"The Moon: A World of Contrasts","paragraphs":["The expression 'dark side of the Moon' often appears in ordinary conversation. It suggests a hemisphere that never receives sunlight. That picture is incorrect. The Moon's far side, which people on Earth do not normally see, experiences day and night as the Moon travels around our planet.","The explanation depends on motion. The Moon rotates on its axis at nearly the same rate that it completes an orbit around Earth. This synchronous rotation keeps approximately the same hemisphere facing our planet. It does not fix one half of the Moon permanently toward the Sun. As the Moon moves, the location of sunlight changes, producing the phases we observe.","The distinction between 'far' and 'dark' may seem small, but precise language matters in science. A misleading phrase can survive because it is memorable, even after observations contradict its literal meaning. Students who check the geometry discover that the far side is not a place of eternal night. They also learn a broader habit: when a familiar label conflicts with evidence, the explanation—not the evidence—must be revised."],"source":"NASA. Moon Facts.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["sailors seek the truth about a supposed sea monster","systematic observation supports asteroid-risk decisions","the contrast among planets shows both the value and limit of labels","the Moon’s motion explains a common error in language"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["planetary-defense scientists","Professor Aronnax, Ned Land, Conseil, and Captain Nemo","Professor Aronnax and an international expedition","Earth’s Moon as observed by scientists"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["the long road to the Emerald City","a remarkable submarine beneath the ocean","the inner and outer solar system","the near and far sides of the Moon"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["the popular phrase dark side of the Moon gives a false impression","scientists need evidence that a spacecraft could alter an asteroid’s movement","the explorers mistake advanced technology for a living creature","simple planet labels can hide major physical differences"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["researchers compare compositions, paths, and impact records","the essay distinguishes a far side from a permanently unlit side","DART deliberately strikes the moonlet Dimorphos in September 2022","they board a vessel built and commanded by Captain Nemo"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["Aronnax admires the science but worries about their lack of choice","the seafloor reveals unexpected natural beauty and danger","monitoring helps officials distinguish low risks from meaningful threats","the Moon’s rotation and orbit explain our stable viewing angle"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the mission tested deflection rather than responding to a known immediate threat","witnesses describe something that moves with surprising speed","he continues to ask for brains even while solving problems","both lunar hemispheres receive sunlight at different times"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured","controlled experiments can inform future safety strategies","familiar expressions can mislead when taken literally","technology can transform the limits of human experience"]},{"question":"In context, what does “synchronous” mean?","choices":["a system for classifying things into related groups","happening at the same rate or in matching timing","the path followed by a moving object","the condition of being held without freedom"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear","scientific terms should correct rather than reinforce misconceptions","exploration depends on tools and disciplined observation","competence is better assessed by evidence than by a label"]}],"answers":["D","D","D","A","B","D","D","C","B","B"]},{"id":"9-1","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":1,"genre":"Informational Passage","title":"Why the Moon Changes Shape","paragraphs":["During one week the Moon may look like a bright crescent; later it may appear round. Nothing has been carved away from its surface. The Moon remains a rocky world. Its changing appearance is caused by sunlight and by the angle from which people on Earth see the illuminated portion.","The Sun lights half of the Moon at a time. As the Moon travels around Earth, our view of that bright half changes. A full moon occurs when the side facing us is brightly illuminated. Near new moon, the side facing Earth receives little direct sunlight. The familiar sequence then repeats.","The Moon also rotates at nearly the same rate that it travels around Earth. For this reason, people on Earth usually see the same lunar hemisphere. Calling the other hemisphere permanently dark is misleading: sunlight reaches it too, just at different times. Understanding these patterns shows why scientists distinguish between an object's real condition and how it appears to an observer. The changing illumination explains the phases rather than any changing shape of the Moon."],"source":"NASA. Moon Facts.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a strange bedroom test determines royal identity","a ruler’s pride allows a deception to spread","an image becomes more powerful than the person it follows","the Moon’s phases depend on sunlight and viewpoint"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["a scholar and his independent shadow","two young people searching for a mysterious bell","Earth and its interacting natural systems","the Moon moving around Earth"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve","a royal guest room","the Earth–Moon system","the third planet from the Sun"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the Moon appears to change shape even though its solid body remains intact","the queen doubts that a rain-soaked visitor is a true princess","the emperor fears admitting that he sees no magnificent cloth","planets differ greatly despite belonging to the same system"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the shadow dresses as an important man and manipulates social opinion","she lights matches and imagines warmth, food, and her grandmother","the explanation follows changing views of its sunlit half","she hides a tiny pea beneath many mattresses"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["readers can distinguish high tide, low tide, and tidal range","moon phases are understood as patterns of illumination","planetary diversity becomes a tool for learning how worlds form","the tale ends tragically, exposing neglect of the poor"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the test is based on an exaggerated sensitivity to a pea","the recognized planets include four inner rocky worlds and four outer giants","the Moon is lit by the Sun and does not produce its own visible light","the sound seems different from each crowded street"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["a test may reveal more about its maker’s beliefs than the person tested","curiosity may lead beyond convenient explanations","apparent changes are not always physical changes","habitable environments depend on several conditions working together"]},{"question":"In context, what does “illumination” mean?","choices":["light falling on an object","sort things into groups using shared features","difficult to explain or understand","the feeling of expecting something to happen"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["status should not replace truth as a standard for judgment","observations should be explained with geometry and evidence","honesty may require courage when a crowd agrees","society has a duty to see people who are left outside"]}],"answers":["D","D","C","A","C","B","C","C","A","B"]},{"id":"9-2","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":2,"genre":"Fable","title":"The Crow and the Pitcher","paragraphs":["A crow flew across a dry village during a hot afternoon. After searching for a long time, she found a pitcher beside a courtyard wall. There was water inside, but the level was too low for her beak to reach. She tipped the pitcher with her claws, yet it was too heavy to move.","The crow did not abandon the water. She studied the ground and noticed small pebbles near a path. Picking up one pebble at a time, she dropped them into the pitcher. Each stone fell to the bottom and pushed the water a little higher.","The work took patience. Finally, the water rose close enough for the crow to drink. She had not made the pitcher smaller or her beak longer. Instead, she used ordinary stones in an unusual way. What first seemed impossible became manageable through observation and effort. The crow was resourceful because she changed her method instead of giving up."],"source":"Aesop. Aesop’s Fables (V. S. Vernon Jones, trans.). Project Gutenberg, eBook 11339.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["false warnings make a real emergency harder to face","a slow competitor wins by refusing to stop","a bird uses pebbles to reach drinking water","a dog loses a real meal while chasing an illusion"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["a patient tortoise and a speedy hare","the North Wind and the Sun","an elderly farmer and his sons","a thirsty crow"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a hot courtyard","a vineyard wall","a hill above a village","a small farm"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the shepherd repeatedly calls for help without danger","the grasshopper prepares no food for the cold season","water lies too low inside a narrow pitcher","grapes hang beyond the fox’s reach"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["he destroys the goose hoping to find more gold inside","the crow drops small stones into the pitcher","they dig the entire vineyard searching for buried treasure","the tortoise walks steadily while the hare naps"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["he loses both the goose and its future eggs","the water rises to the bird’s beak","the tortoise reaches the finish first","winter finds the ants prepared and the grasshopper hungry"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the ants work when food is plentiful","the father mentions treasure hidden among the vines","the image in the stream is only a reflection","each pebble displaces some water"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["wanting more can cause a person to lose what is already theirs","people may criticize what they cannot obtain","steady effort can overcome careless talent","careful thinking can solve a practical problem"]},{"question":"In context, what does “resourceful” mean?","choices":["make ready for something that will happen","able to use available things cleverly","an image seen in water or glass","speak too proudly about an ability"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["work can be more valuable than a hidden fortune","dishonesty harms both the speaker and others","ingenuity can achieve what force cannot","kindness can be more effective than pressure"]}],"answers":["C","D","A","C","B","B","D","D","B","C"]},{"id":"9-3","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":3,"genre":"Fiction","title":"The Frog Prince","paragraphs":["A princess played with her golden ball beside a deep pool. When the ball slipped from her hands and sank, she began to cry. A frog offered to retrieve it if she promised to treat him as a companion at the palace. Desperate to recover her toy, she agreed.","The frog brought the ball to the surface, but the princess hurried home without him. Later he appeared at the palace door and reminded her of her promise. She was embarrassed to be seen with a muddy frog. Her father, the king, insisted that she should honor a promise freely made.","The princess reluctantly allowed the frog inside. In the familiar fairy-tale ending, the enchantment that had transformed a prince into a frog was broken. The story does more than celebrate magic. It shows how easy it is to offer a promise when we want help and how difficult it can be to fulfill that promise when circumstances change. The princess discovered that her obligation did not disappear when she disliked the result."],"source":"Grimm, J., & Grimm, W. Grimms’ Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 2591.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2591","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a rescue succeeds because four talents fit together","a child’s honest imitation changes family behavior","secret help leads to grateful giving","a difficult promise tests a princess’s honesty"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["an elderly grandfather and a small child","a princess and an enchanted frog","a servant who understands animals","a cat and a boastful fox"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a forest path","a palace beside a deep pool","a castle with a secret underground path","a kingdom threatened by a dragon"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the fox trusts many clever tricks while danger approaches","a princess is captured and no single brother can rescue her","the king cannot explain his daughters’ worn dancing shoes","the princess loses a golden ball and makes a promise she regrets"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["they cooperate to frighten robbers out of a cottage","a frog retrieves the ball and asks her to keep her word","the youngest prince protects creatures who later assist him","the child copies the adults by preparing a wooden bowl for their future"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["they save the princess through teamwork","his odd procession makes a princess laugh","the helpers receive clothing and the shoemaker rebuilds his livelihood","the broken spell reveals a prince"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["their combined cries sound terrifying in the darkness","branches of silver and gold mark their hidden route","the mysterious shoes are sewn with extraordinary skill","the princess initially refuses to welcome her unlikely helper"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["children learn conduct by watching adults","specialized talents become powerful when coordinated","one useful skill can be better than many empty claims","promises reveal character when keeping them is inconvenient"]},{"question":"In context, what does “obligation” mean?","choices":["skill in making things carefully","the respect owed to every person","unkind laughter at another person","a responsibility created by a promise or duty"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["compassion is linked to clever problem-solving","gratitude requires action rather than pleasant words","shared success need not belong to one person","generosity can create a circle of goodwill"]}],"answers":["D","B","B","D","B","D","D","D","D","B"]},{"id":"9-4","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":4,"genre":"Bible Scripture","title":"David and Goliath: Courage with Preparation","paragraphs":["An army faced a frightening challenge from Goliath, a warrior whose size and weapons intimidated the opposing soldiers. Day after day he called for someone to fight him. David, a young shepherd visiting his brothers, heard the challenge. He believed that fear should not determine whether people defended what they understood to be right.","When David offered to confront the warrior, King Saul at first doubted his ability. David explained that, while protecting sheep, he had already faced dangerous animals. Saul offered him heavy armor, but David could not move comfortably in it. Instead, he took a sling and selected smooth stones from a stream.","The confrontation ended quickly. David used the skill he had practiced rather than copying another soldier's equipment. The biblical narrative interprets the victory through trust in God, but it also gives readers a concrete contrast: powerful weapons are not identical with wise preparation. David's action joins conviction, practice, and decision under pressure. Readers may consider how bravery differs from acting without thought."],"source":"1 Samuel 17:1–50; original classroom paraphrase (KJV)","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best captures the central teaching of this scripture-based reading?","choices":["Loyalty can require choosing companionship despite uncertainty","Courage can draw on practiced skill rather than appearance","Responsible courage may involve calculated personal risk","Compassion is demonstrated by helping a person in need"]},{"question":"Which person or group is most central to the account?","choices":["the reflective speaker of Ecclesiastes","Esther, the queen","the Samaritan who helped the traveler","David, the young shepherd"]},{"question":"What context or event establishes the situation?","choices":["a sequence of contrasting seasons in human life","an army fears Goliath’s repeated challenge","Naomi prepares to return to Bethlehem","Mordecai warns Esther about a threat to her people"]},{"question":"Which tension or problem drives the account?","choices":["soldiers are intimidated by a heavily armed opponent","approaching the king uninvited is dangerous","the audience does not share every premise of Paul’s belief","Naomi has lost her husband and sons"]},{"question":"Which important action or decision is described?","choices":["the Samaritan stops and treats the wounds","the speaker sets paired experiences beside one another","David selects a sling and smooth stones","Paul refers to an altar dedicated to an unknown god"]},{"question":"What result or response follows?","choices":["David defeats the warrior in the confrontation","the reflection encourages humility and appreciation","listeners react with mockery, curiosity, or belief","the innkeeper receives money to continue care"]},{"question":"Which detail gives specific support to the account?","choices":["Naomi releases her daughters-in-law from obligation","Esther asks her community to fast before she acts","an existing inscription becomes a point of contact","David had defended sheep from dangerous animals"]},{"question":"What inference about motive or meaning is most justified?","choices":["careful timing can strengthen a difficult appeal","David trusts experience and conviction over unfamiliar armor","Ruth values belonging and faithfulness more than a promised reward","describing life’s changes is not endorsing every possible act"]},{"question":"Which phrase best explains a significant word or idea in the passage?","choices":["mercy means compassionate help toward someone suffering","pessimistic means emphasizing discouraging aspects or limits","commitment means a lasting promise shown in conduct","intimidated means made fearful or discouraged"]},{"question":"Which broader interpretation is most consistent with the passage?","choices":["the apparent opposites create a nuanced rather than simple lesson","the story respects grief while showing the strength of solidarity","the narrative connects faith and preparation rather than treating courage as noise","the mixed response shows the difference between a strong argument and universal acceptance"]}],"answers":["B","D","B","A","C","A","D","B","D","C"]},{"id":"9-5","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":5,"genre":"Novel","title":"The Locked Garden Door","paragraphs":["Mary Lennox arrives at a large house in Yorkshire after losing her parents. At first, she finds the manor gloomy and its people distant. The corridors seem too wide, the rooms too quiet, and the adults too busy to explain much. Yet the gardens outside suggest that the house contains stories she has not heard.","A servant mentions a walled garden that has been locked for years. Its owner cannot bear to enter it because it reminds him of a family tragedy. Mary becomes fascinated by the mystery. She studies the paths, listens to the robin singing near the walls, and looks for signs of an entrance. Her interest gives her a purpose beyond complaining about her new surroundings.","The garden is more than a puzzle about a key. It is connected with sorrow, memory, and the possibility of renewal. As Mary explores, she begins to observe living things and other people more closely. That change in attention prepares the way for a change in her character. The secluded garden began to represent the possibility of change."],"source":"Burnett, F. H. The Secret Garden. Project Gutenberg, eBook 113.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/113","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["Alice follows a curious clue into an unfamiliar world","a tea party turns ordinary conversation into a puzzle","Dorothy begins a journey to find her way home","a hidden garden becomes the center of Mary’s change"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["Mary Lennox, a lonely child","Tom Sawyer and his neighborhood friends","Alice, an inquisitive young girl","Dorothy, a girl far from home"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a hallway flooded with Alice’s tears","the unfamiliar Land of Oz","a wooden fence outside Aunt Polly’s home","a large Yorkshire manor and its grounds"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names","a white rabbit behaves in a way that defies ordinary expectations","Mary hears of a garden kept locked for years","conversation does not follow the reasonable rules Alice expects"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the essay compares rotation with revolution","the article compares icy, volcanic, and atmosphere-rich satellites","she listens, observes, and searches for a hidden key","he makes the work appear like a special privilege"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["he joins the journey and begins showing practical judgment","she must learn that her normal assumptions do not always help","her curiosity begins to draw her into nature and friendship","her journey begins with a goal and uncertainty about the path"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["tidal range measures the difference between high and low water","the table companions argue about riddles and time","the yellow brick road offers direction but not a guarantee of safety","the garden was closed after a painful family loss"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["hope becomes practical when combined with action","places can hold memories as well as physical objects","not every confident answer is logically sound","human calendars are models adjusted to natural cycles"]},{"question":"In context, what does “secluded” mean?","choices":["kept apart from other people or places","a prediction about conditions expected later","confused by surprising events","the act of influencing someone’s choice through words or example"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["wonder can disturb our assumptions about ordinary life","cleverness can change a social situation but deserves ethical scrutiny","discovery often begins with patient attention","accurate measurement sometimes requires practical adjustment"]}],"answers":["D","A","D","C","C","C","D","B","A","C"]},{"id":"9-6","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":6,"genre":"Short Story","title":"The Fir Tree","paragraphs":["A little fir tree grew among taller trees in a forest. Sunlight warmed its needles, birds rested on its branches, and children sometimes passed nearby. Yet the tree did not enjoy these things. It wished to become taller and more important. When woodcutters carried away the larger trees, it imagined that they were beginning a wonderful journey.","Eventually people cut the fir tree and placed it in a bright room. They covered it with ornaments, sweets, and candles. For one evening the tree stood at the center of a celebration. It believed an even greater future would follow. Instead, it was carried to a dark attic, where it waited through long, silent days.","In the darkness the tree remembered the forest more warmly than it had ever experienced it. The birds, sunshine, and freedom now seemed precious. Its mistake had not been hoping for something good. It had been treating every present moment as unimportant while expecting happiness to begin somewhere else. The tree’s anticipation of future glory made the present seem dull."],"source":"Andersen, H. C. Andersen’s Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1597.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1597","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["a strange bedroom test determines royal identity","a tree learns too late to value its life in the forest","water, air, and temperature make Earth distinctive","comparison reveals both patterns and exceptions among planets"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["a poor girl selling matches","a vain emperor and two dishonest weavers","the Moon moving around Earth","a young fir tree"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a forest and a decorated house","a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve","a royal guest room","a city where appearances influence reputation"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["the tree is unhappy with its present life and longs for the future","the emperor fears admitting that he sees no magnificent cloth","the queen doubts that a rain-soaked visitor is a true princess","readers must explain why Earth supports familiar forms of life"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["she lights matches and imagines warmth, food, and her grandmother","she hides a tiny pea beneath many mattresses","the article compares rocky inner planets with outer giants","it waits eagerly to grow tall and become a Christmas tree"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["moon phases are understood as patterns of illumination","after the celebration it is forgotten and its earlier happiness is lost","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","Earth emerges as a dynamic system rather than a simple rocky ball"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the sound seems different from each crowded street","the tree barely notices sunshine or birds while imagining later glory","the recognized planets include four inner rocky worlds and four outer giants","the Moon is lit by the Sun and does not produce its own visible light"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["social pressure can make many people repeat a lie","curiosity may lead beyond convenient explanations","predictable natural cycles can still affect human choices","constant longing may hide the value of the present"]},{"question":"In context, what does “anticipation” mean?","choices":["suitable for living things","difficult to explain or understand","sort things into groups using shared features","the feeling of expecting something to happen"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["status should not replace truth as a standard for judgment","similar origins can produce very different environments","environmental systems are interconnected","appreciating ordinary moments can prevent regret"]}],"answers":["B","D","A","A","D","B","B","D","D","D"]},{"id":"9-7","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":7,"genre":"Essay","title":"The Social Cost of Pretending","paragraphs":["An entire crowd can become reluctant to name what it sees. In Hans Christian Andersen's tale about an emperor and imaginary clothes, the danger begins when people fear appearing foolish. Each observer assumes that admitting uncertainty will reveal a personal weakness. Silence becomes contagious, and a private doubt turns into a public deception.","This pattern is not confined to fairy tales. A school group may accept an inaccurate answer because everyone believes somebody else has already checked it. A committee may repeat an impressive claim without demanding its source. Agreement can look like evidence even when no one has independently examined the facts.","The solution is not to distrust every shared judgment. Communities need cooperation and reasonable confidence in others. Yet cooperation becomes fragile when members are discouraged from asking for verification. The child who identifies the emperor's condition performs a simple but important task: the child separates observation from reputation. The essay suggests that intellectual honesty requires the courage to question a claim politely, with reasons, especially when social pressure makes pretending easier than examining."],"source":"Original analytical essay based on Andersen, The Emperor’s New Clothes","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1597","questions":[{"question":"Which statement is the essay’s primary claim?","choices":["An experiment should distinguish a measured result from a universal claim","Social pressure can make obvious errors seem acceptable","A trustworthy inference considers context and alternatives","Rereading helps a reader notice reasons and supporting details"]},{"question":"Which example does the writer use to clarify the claim?","choices":["the gardener and student examples","the example of a remembered fable","an object discovered near a crime","the crowd in the emperor’s tale"]},{"question":"Which difficulty or mistaken belief is discussed?","choices":["uncertainty about applying one test to every asteroid","fear of being judged foolish","the risk of treating one observation as proof","the limits of attention during a first reading"]},{"question":"Which contrast is used to develop the argument?","choices":["shared agreement contrasted with independent observation","uncritical acceptance versus automatic rejection","waiting without action versus purposeful persistence","objectives and observations contrasted with interpretation"]},{"question":"Which supporting action or reasoning appears in the essay?","choices":["the investigator compares observations with new facts","the reader evaluates observations, uncertainty, and alternatives","a child states what others refuse to acknowledge","the tortoise keeps moving toward the finish"]},{"question":"What consequence or outcome does the writer identify?","choices":["honest verification can interrupt a public illusion","multiple consistent details strengthen a conclusion","steady practice can support long-term improvement","the result informs but does not settle planetary-defense questions"]},{"question":"Which detail most directly supports the argument?","choices":["a famous institution is not itself a complete proof","the tortoise would not succeed by staying at the start","students first track unfamiliar words and names","people repeat claims without checking sources"]},{"question":"What inference follows from the writer’s reasoning?","choices":["habits can be controlled more directly than outcomes","consensus alone does not establish factual truth","stronger claims ordinarily demand stronger supporting evidence","a plausible alternative must be tested rather than ignored"]},{"question":"Which phrase most accurately explains a key term from the essay?","choices":["autonomy means self-directed responsible judgment","investigation means a careful search for answers and evidence","restraint means controlling an impulse or reaction","contagious means spreading from one person to another"]},{"question":"Which conclusion best respects the essay’s qualifications?","choices":["the best interpretation separates narrative excitement from reliable proof","intellectual humility and critical independence can coexist","questioning a claim respectfully may protect a community from error","scientific confidence grows when conclusions include explicit limits"]}],"answers":["B","D","B","A","C","A","D","B","D","C"]},{"id":"9-8","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":8,"genre":"News Report","title":"Asteroid Bennu Sample Reaches Earth","paragraphs":["September 2023 — A small capsule carrying pieces of asteroid Bennu returned to Earth after a long NASA mission. The OSIRIS-REx spacecraft had collected rocky material from Bennu in October 2020. On September 24, 2023, its return capsule landed in Utah, allowing scientists to begin careful study of the sample.","Bennu is an asteroid, not a planet. Researchers are interested in its dust and rocks because such material can preserve clues from the early solar system. To protect the sample from contamination, specialists transferred it to facilities designed for careful handling.","The mission required years of planning, travel, collection, and recovery. Its headline result was not the discovery of life on Bennu. Rather, the return of physical material gave laboratories an opportunity to investigate chemical and geological questions with instruments on Earth. News readers should separate the concrete achievement of a sample return from broader hypotheses about the origins of planets and the ingredients needed for life."],"source":"Original retrospective news report based on NASA OSIRIS-REx mission records","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/osiris-rex/","questions":[{"question":"What event is the main focus of this retrospective science report?","choices":["an intentional spacecraft impact tests a defense idea","a NASA mission returned material from asteroid Bennu","NASA completed an uncrewed Moon-flight test with Orion","systematic observation supports asteroid-risk decisions"]},{"question":"Which institution or equipment is central to the event?","choices":["coastal residents using tidal information","the James Webb Space Telescope and NASA","planetary-defense scientists","NASA’s OSIRIS-REx spacecraft and sample capsule"]},{"question":"Which date or time period is reported?","choices":["space near the asteroid system Didymos","September 24, 2023","November 16 to December 11, 2022","observatories on Earth and in space"]},{"question":"What was the principal purpose of the work?","choices":["bring asteroid material to Earth for study","show the telescope’s observing capabilities","a rising tide can surprise people who ignore local conditions","some near-Earth objects could eventually pose an impact hazard"]},{"question":"Which action took place?","choices":["Webb collected infrared-related observations","residents consult tidal predictions before planning activities","the capsule landed in Utah after sample collection","scientists locate, track, and refine the predicted paths of objects"]},{"question":"What reported outcome occurred?","choices":["scientists received protected samples for laboratory analysis","monitoring helps officials distinguish low risks from meaningful threats","measurements show that the impact changed the moonlet’s orbit","the spacecraft splashed down in the Pacific Ocean"]},{"question":"Which detail supplies evidence for the report?","choices":["NASA coordinates planetary-defense observations and warnings","tidal range measures the difference between high and low water","SMACS 0723 occupied a very small patch of sky","the rocks had been collected in October 2020"]},{"question":"Why is this event considered significant?","choices":["the success was a preparation step rather than a new Moon landing","samples may preserve clues about the early solar system","risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured","beautiful images also support measurement and research"]},{"question":"Which option defines a technical word used in the report?","choices":["a change in the direction or course of movement","the path followed by a moving object","infrared refers to light wavelengths beyond visible red","contamination means unwanted material entering a sample"]},{"question":"What limitation or caution should a careful reader keep in mind?","choices":["a careful report distinguishes a test from a finished long-term program","credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear","retrieving a sample does not by itself prove the origin of life","planning requires translating evidence into decisions"]}],"answers":["B","D","B","A","C","A","D","B","D","C"]},{"id":"9-9","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":9,"genre":"Poetry","title":"The Lantern in the Library","paragraphs":["Between two shelves a lantern glows,\nIts amber circle small but clear.\nIt cannot tell me all it knows;\nIt shows the sentence waiting here.","One book describes a distant shore,\nAnother asks why cities rise.\nA page can open one small door,\nYet not replace the reader's eyes.","I mark a claim; I test a date.\nI notice what the writer leaves.\nA patient question learns to wait\nBefore it calls a thought belief.","At closing time the room is dark.\nI carry neither shelf nor flame;\nBut in my notes a careful mark\nReminds me why I read the same."],"source":"Original classroom poem about evidence and reading; literary reference: Frost, Selected Poems","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/59824","questions":[{"question":"What is the central idea expressed by the poem?","choices":["A storm can change how a person perceives ordinary life","Knowledge grows through questioning rather than mere possession of books","Complex events often have multiple contributing causes","Small, steady progress offers a lesson for living"]},{"question":"Which image is central to its meaning?","choices":["rain drawing lines on a window","a changing sea and its shoreline","a creek moving beside a gate","a lantern between library shelves"]},{"question":"What is the main setting or situation in the poem?","choices":["a map considered against a changing physical world","a library during an evening reading session","the path and a flowing stream","a dim room looking toward a street at dusk"]},{"question":"Which change is experienced or observed by the speaker?","choices":["the reader begins to check claims and dates","a leaf moves while a pebble stays still","the speaker moves from certainty toward inquiry","the speaker first wants one simple explanation"]},{"question":"Which contrast or comparison shapes the imagery?","choices":["the moon and local wind are contrasted","old light is compared with information from the past","light represents partial guidance rather than complete knowledge","satellite measurement contrasts with lived experience"]},{"question":"What emotional or intellectual movement does the poem show?","choices":["the reader leaves with notes and questions","the creek continues after the speaker goes home","the morning appears refreshed","the reader gains a more qualified trust in models"]},{"question":"Which textual detail most clearly supports an interpretation?","choices":["the road seems newly colored after the storm","waves and tide provide evidence of ongoing change","the speaker writes a figure and also a question","the lantern illuminates only a small circle"]},{"question":"What might the central image or situation suggest?","choices":["the ocean symbolizes the limits of simple answers","a text is useful when the reader examines it independently","distant astronomy reveals limits of immediate perception","the changing weather parallels a change in attention"]},{"question":"Which option best explains one important word or expression?","choices":["renewal means a sense of freshness or beginning again","convention means an agreed method of representing something","sufficient means enough to explain or satisfy a need","claim means a statement offered as true"]},{"question":"How should the closing lines be understood?","choices":["the final stanza favors evidence over false certainty","the final color image suggests changed perception rather than magic","the ending values careful thinking more than collecting facts","the ending suggests that learning can continue beyond one walk"]}],"answers":["B","D","B","A","C","A","D","B","D","C"]},{"id":"9-10","grade":9,"color":"Amber","num":10,"genre":"Science & Nonfiction","title":"Asteroids: Fragments with Histories","paragraphs":["Asteroids are small bodies that orbit the Sun, but they are not all the same kind of rock. Some contain much carbon-rich material, others consist largely of silicate minerals, and some are rich in metal. Many are found in the broad region between Mars and Jupiter, although their paths can be changed by gravitational encounters.","Their irregular shapes and battered surfaces tell stories of collision and fragmentation. Some pieces preserve materials from early stages of the solar system. Scientists use observations of reflected light, radar, spacecraft measurements, and studies of meteorites to investigate their composition and motion. Each method provides only part of the answer; together they create a stronger picture.","Studying asteroids is therefore both an exercise in classification and a historical investigation. A rock that appears insignificant from a distance may hold evidence about how planets began to grow. At the same time, understanding orbital paths is important because some objects travel near Earth. A single scientific subject can connect basic research with public safety."],"source":"NASA. Asteroid Facts.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["an underwater journey changes the scientist’s sense of nature","the Moon’s motion explains a common error in language","an intentional spacecraft impact tests a defense idea","asteroids are diverse records of planetary history"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["Professor Aronnax, Ned Land, Conseil, and Captain Nemo","Professor Aronnax and an international expedition","NASA’s DART mission and an asteroid moonlet","asteroids and the scientists who study them"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["the near and far sides of the Moon","orbits within and beyond the main asteroid belt","the long road to the Emerald City","observatories on Earth and in space"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["simple planet labels can hide major physical differences","small rocky bodies are often mistaken for identical debris","some near-Earth objects could eventually pose an impact hazard","the popular phrase dark side of the Moon gives a false impression"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["Aronnax joins a voyage to investigate the reports","researchers compare compositions, paths, and impact records","they board a vessel built and commanded by Captain Nemo","scientists locate, track, and refine the predicted paths of objects"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the supposed sea monster is revealed as the submarine Nautilus","asteroids reveal information about the solar system’s formation","classification helps inquiry but must be revised when needed","Aronnax admires the science but worries about their lack of choice"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the mission tested deflection rather than responding to a known immediate threat","many asteroids orbit between Mars and Jupiter","Nemo’s independence depends on living apart from ordinary society","both lunar hemispheres receive sunlight at different times"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["technology can transform the limits of human experience","classification can reveal a history concealed by appearance","familiar expressions can mislead when taken literally","risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured"]},{"question":"In context, what does “composition” mean?","choices":["the materials from which something is made","the path followed by a moving object","the discovery of something previously hidden","a proposed explanation that can be investigated"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["exploration depends on tools and disciplined observation","small objects can preserve important evidence about the past","preparation is more reliable when methods are tested beforehand","scientific terms should correct rather than reinforce misconceptions"]}],"answers":["D","D","B","B","B","B","B","B","A","B"]},{"id":"10-1","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":1,"genre":"Informational Passage","title":"The Ocean Rises and Falls","paragraphs":["A shoreline does not stay at exactly the same water level throughout the day. At some times the sea reaches far up the beach; at others, wet sand and rocks become visible. These regular rises and falls are called tides. The highest stage at a location is high tide, and the lowest stage is low tide.","The Moon's gravitational pull is a major cause of ocean tides. The Sun also contributes. Together with the motion of Earth, these forces produce long-period waves that travel through ocean basins and appear as changing water levels along coasts. The height difference between high and low tide is called the tidal range.","Tide patterns matter beyond a science classroom. Fishers, boat operators, coastal residents, and people exploring tidal flats may need to know when water will rise. Even though tides follow regular natural influences, local coastlines can produce different timing and heights. Careful observation and forecasts help communities plan around a moving ocean."],"source":"National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA). What Are Tides?","sourceUrl":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/tides.html","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a distant bell invites a search for deeper meaning","the tides are recurring movements shaped by gravity","an image becomes more powerful than the person it follows","a strange bedroom test determines royal identity"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["two young people searching for a mysterious bell","the ocean, Moon, and Sun","Earth and its interacting natural systems","the Moon moving around Earth"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a royal guest room","an imperial city during a public parade","coastlines around Earth","a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["coastal water levels change in a repeating pattern","people hear a distant sound but cannot agree where it comes from","the Moon appears to change shape even though its solid body remains intact","the queen doubts that a rain-soaked visitor is a true princess"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the article connects tidal motion to gravitational forces","she lights matches and imagines warmth, food, and her grandmother","the article connects surface water, temperature, and atmosphere","the explanation follows changing views of its sunlit half"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the guest reports an uncomfortable night and is accepted","readers can distinguish high tide, low tide, and tidal range","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","after the celebration it is forgotten and its earlier happiness is lost"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the Moon is lit by the Sun and does not produce its own visible light","the Moon and Sun both exert gravitational pulls related to tides","the tree barely notices sunshine or birds while imagining later glory","each brief flame produces a comforting vision"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["social pressure can make many people repeat a lie","curiosity may lead beyond convenient explanations","apparent changes are not always physical changes","predictable natural cycles can still affect human choices"]},{"question":"In context, what does “tidal range” mean?","choices":["an imagined or dreamlike sight","not easily convinced without evidence","the height difference between high and low tide","light falling on an object"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["knowing natural patterns improves safety and planning","understanding may demand patient exploration","similar origins can produce very different environments","observations should be explained with geometry and evidence"]}],"answers":["B","B","C","A","A","B","B","D","C","A"]},{"id":"10-2","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":2,"genre":"Fable","title":"The North Wind and the Sun","paragraphs":["The North Wind and the Sun argued about which of them was more powerful. They noticed a traveler walking along a road with a heavy coat. They agreed that whoever made the traveler remove the coat would win their contest.","The North Wind tried first. He blew so fiercely that dust rose from the road and branches shook. But the traveler only pulled his coat more tightly around his shoulders. The harder the wind blew, the more determined the traveler became to keep warm.","Then the Sun appeared from behind a cloud. Its steady warmth spread over the path. The traveler relaxed, wiped his forehead, and finally removed his coat. The Sun had not pushed or frightened him. Warmth changed his choice more effectively than violence had. The story suggests that gentle persuasion may achieve what pressure cannot. The Sun managed to persuade the traveler without using force."],"source":"Aesop. Aesop’s Fables (V. S. Vernon Jones, trans.). Project Gutenberg, eBook 11339.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["the sun wins a contest by giving warmth","a slow competitor wins by refusing to stop","a small creature repays a powerful one","false warnings make a real emergency harder to face"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["the North Wind and the Sun","working ants and a singing grasshopper","a small mouse and a lion","an elderly farmer and his sons"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a road beneath the sky","a hill above a village","a vineyard wall","a bridge over a stream"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the grasshopper prepares no food for the cold season","grapes hang beyond the fox’s reach","two forces argue about which is stronger","a lion dismisses a mouse as too weak to help"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the crow drops small stones into the pitcher","the ants carry supplies into their home","the wind blows hard but the sun shines gently","he destroys the goose hoping to find more gold inside"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the fox pretends the grapes were not worth eating","he loses both the goose and its future eggs","its own food falls into the water","a traveler removes his coat under the sun’s warmth"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the traveler grips his coat tighter during the storm","the father mentions treasure hidden among the vines","the image in the stream is only a reflection","the bunch remains untouched above his head"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["a truthful warning depends on earned trust","planning protects people when conditions change","greed can ruin a reliable good fortune","gentle influence can succeed where force fails"]},{"question":"In context, what does “persuade” mean?","choices":["sharp or unpleasant in taste","kind treatment of someone in your power","make ready for something that will happen","lead someone to act without forcing them"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["kindness can be more effective than pressure","contentment guards against needless loss","work can be more valuable than a hidden fortune","ingenuity can achieve what force cannot"]}],"answers":["A","A","A","C","C","D","A","D","D","A"]},{"id":"10-3","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":3,"genre":"Fiction","title":"The Twelve Dancing Princesses","paragraphs":["Every morning, the shoes of twelve princesses were worn through, although their bedroom had been locked at night. Their father offered a reward to anyone who could explain the mystery. Many visitors failed because they fell asleep before anything happened.","One traveler received help from an old woman, who warned him not to drink a sleeping potion. He pretended to sleep and then followed the princesses while wearing an invisible cloak. Beneath the bedroom floor, the sisters passed through a secret doorway and descended into a magical world.","They crossed forests with glittering leaves and traveled over dark water to a palace filled with dancing. The traveler collected evidence from the unusual trees and watched the sisters return before morning. When questioned, he described their route and showed what he had brought back. His answer depended not on guessing, but on observing details that other people had missed."],"source":"Grimm, J., & Grimm, W. Grimms’ Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 2591.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2591","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["four unwanted animals find safety as a team","a difficult promise tests a princess’s honesty","a traveler solves the mystery of worn-out shoes","secret help leads to grateful giving"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["twelve princesses and a watchful traveler","an elderly grandfather and a small child","a kind younger brother called Simpleton","a cat and a boastful fox"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a castle with a secret underground path","a forest and an enchanted castle","a road toward Bremen and a forest cottage","a forest and a royal town"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["the brothers threaten small animals and cannot solve castle tasks","the shoemaker has leather for only one final pair","the fox trusts many clever tricks while danger approaches","the king cannot explain his daughters’ worn dancing shoes"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["the child copies the adults by preparing a wooden bowl for their future","the traveler stays awake and follows the princesses invisibly","they cooperate to frighten robbers out of a cottage","the youngest prince protects creatures who later assist him"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the cat survives while the fox cannot choose a trick quickly enough","he reveals the secret midnight dances","his odd procession makes a princess laugh","the helpers receive clothing and the shoemaker rebuilds his livelihood"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the mysterious shoes are sewn with extraordinary skill","the servant gains his unusual gift after tasting a white snake","branches of silver and gold mark their hidden route","the cat knows one reliable escape"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["specialized talents become powerful when coordinated","cooperation can make vulnerable individuals stronger","care for vulnerable lives may bring unexpected allies","careful observation can uncover an unlikely explanation"]},{"question":"In context, what does “invisible” mean?","choices":["changed by a magical power","unable to be seen","too eager to praise one’s own abilities","the respect owed to every person"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["compassion is linked to clever problem-solving","evidence is more useful than a convenient guess","gratitude requires action rather than pleasant words","shared success need not belong to one person"]}],"answers":["C","A","A","D","B","B","C","D","B","B"]},{"id":"10-4","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":4,"genre":"Bible Scripture","title":"Esther: A Risk Taken for Others","paragraphs":["Esther lived in a royal court where a ruler's decisions could alter the lives of entire communities. Her relative Mordecai learned of a plan that threatened the Jewish people and urged Esther to speak. Her position as queen gave her access to power, but approaching the king without an invitation could carry severe consequences.","Esther did not rush into the royal room without preparation. She asked her community to fast, gathered her courage, and chose an appropriate moment to seek an audience. Rather than immediately accusing a powerful official, she arranged a meal at which she could reveal the danger. Her timing made it possible to tell the king not only that an order existed, but also whom that order would harm.","The plot was exposed, and the threatened community gained protection. The narrative invites readers to examine a difficult form of responsibility: a person who holds some influence may still face serious risk when defending others. Esther acts neither as an all-powerful ruler nor as a helpless observer. She uses limited power with deliberate judgment, showing that courage can be strategic rather than impulsive."],"source":"Esther 4–7; original classroom paraphrase (KJV)","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best captures the central teaching of this scripture-based reading?","choices":["Effective argument may begin with shared evidence and then challenge assumptions","Loyalty can require choosing companionship despite uncertainty","Responsible courage may involve calculated personal risk","Wisdom includes recognizing both meaningful action and human limits"]},{"question":"Which person or group is most central to the account?","choices":["Esther, the queen","Ruth and her mother-in-law Naomi","the Samaritan who helped the traveler","Paul speaking to listeners in Athens"]},{"question":"What context or event establishes the situation?","choices":["an army fears Goliath’s repeated challenge","a city filled with public debate and religious images","Mordecai warns Esther about a threat to her people","a sequence of contrasting seasons in human life"]},{"question":"Which tension or problem drives the account?","choices":["Naomi has lost her husband and sons","approaching the king uninvited is dangerous","soldiers are intimidated by a heavily armed opponent","a traveler is hurt and abandoned by robbers"]},{"question":"Which important action or decision is described?","choices":["the speaker sets paired experiences beside one another","David selects a sling and smooth stones","the Samaritan stops and treats the wounds","Esther prepares and arranges a meal to reveal the plot"]},{"question":"What result or response follows?","choices":["the reflection encourages humility and appreciation","the threatened community gains protection","the two women arrive together in Bethlehem","the innkeeper receives money to continue care"]},{"question":"Which detail gives specific support to the account?","choices":["Esther asks her community to fast before she acts","Naomi releases her daughters-in-law from obligation","work brings satisfaction but not permanent certainty","David had defended sheep from dangerous animals"]},{"question":"What inference about motive or meaning is most justified?","choices":["being a neighbor is shown through actions rather than labels","describing life’s changes is not endorsing every possible act","careful timing can strengthen a difficult appeal","Ruth values belonging and faithfulness more than a promised reward"]},{"question":"Which phrase best explains a significant word or idea in the passage?","choices":["deliberate means planned with careful thought","rhetorical means concerned with persuasive communication","mercy means compassionate help toward someone suffering","commitment means a lasting promise shown in conduct"]},{"question":"Which broader interpretation is most consistent with the passage?","choices":["the apparent opposites create a nuanced rather than simple lesson","the narrative connects faith and preparation rather than treating courage as noise","the parable challenges prejudice about who may act kindly","the narrative links influence to an obligation toward vulnerable people"]}],"answers":["C","A","C","B","D","B","A","C","A","D"]},{"id":"10-5","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":5,"genre":"Novel","title":"Tom Sawyer and the Whitewashed Fence","paragraphs":["Tom Sawyer receives a punishment that seems designed to ruin his pleasant Saturday: he must whitewash a long wooden fence. The work is tiring, and the thought of friends passing by on their way to play makes it worse. When another boy approaches, Tom changes his behavior. Instead of complaining, he paints with careful concentration, stepping back to admire each stroke.","The boy asks why Tom is working so hard. Tom responds as though the job were a rare opportunity that required special skill. Gradually the visitor begins to envy him. When Tom reluctantly allows the boy to try, he receives a small treasure in exchange. Other children arrive and make similar bargains, until much of the fence has been painted without Tom doing all the labor.","The humor depends on an unexpected reversal: the same chore seems unpleasant when assigned and desirable when described as exclusive. Twain also leaves a question about fairness. Tom's cleverness entertains the reader, but he deliberately manipulates his friends. Understanding a character's success does not require approving every method he uses. Tom’s persuasion altered how his friends valued the very same work."],"source":"Twain, M. The Adventures of Tom Sawyer. Project Gutenberg, eBook 74.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/74","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["tide knowledge helps people choose safer times","the Scarecrow searches for a quality he already displays","a hidden garden becomes the center of Mary’s change","Tom turns an unwanted chore into an object of desire"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["coastal residents using tidal information","Dorothy and the Scarecrow","Earth rotating and orbiting the Sun","Tom Sawyer and his neighborhood friends"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["different regions of the solar system","a wooden fence outside Aunt Polly’s home","a cornfield beside the yellow brick road","a busy coastal community"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the familiar image of Earth’s Moon does not fit every moon","Mary hears of a garden kept locked for years","Tom must paint a fence while other children are free to play","a white rabbit behaves in a way that defies ordinary expectations"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["Dorothy invites him to travel to the Wizard","he makes the work appear like a special privilege","she chooses to travel toward the Emerald City to seek advice","she listens, observes, and searches for a hidden key"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["her curiosity begins to draw her into nature and friendship","she enters a strange world where normal rules seem unreliable","her journey begins with a goal and uncertainty about the path","other boys trade small treasures for a chance to paint"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["tears shed while she was enormous later form a pool","Tom convinces others that painting requires unusual skill","Earth takes about 365.25 days to complete one orbit","the rabbit checks a pocket watch while hurrying"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["scientific information is most useful when joined with local judgment","curiosity can lead to discovery but also uncertainty","the perceived value of an activity can depend on how it is presented","new environments can make familiar behavior ineffective"]},{"question":"In context, what does “persuasion” mean?","choices":["the act of influencing someone’s choice through words or example","a statement that conflicts with another statement","the ability to make sensible decisions","kept apart from other people or places"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["wonder can disturb our assumptions about ordinary life","adaptability begins with recognizing changed circumstances","discovery often begins with patient attention","cleverness can change a social situation but deserves ethical scrutiny"]}],"answers":["D","D","B","C","B","D","B","C","A","D"]},{"id":"10-6","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":6,"genre":"Short Story","title":"The Little Match Girl","paragraphs":["On a bitterly cold New Year's Eve, a small girl walked through crowded streets carrying matches to sell. Her shoes were gone, her hands trembled, and she feared going home with no money. People hurried past toward warm houses, where lights and the smell of food suggested celebrations she could not share.","She crouched beside a wall and struck one match. For a moment the flame seemed to become a glowing stove. She lit another and imagined a table covered with a delicious meal. A third brought the picture of a beautiful Christmas tree. Each vision vanished when its match burned out.","At last she imagined her beloved grandmother, who had once made her feel safe. The original tale ends with the girl's death in the cold. Her visions express longing, but they do not erase the painful conditions around her. The contrast between warmth inside and suffering outside turns a seemingly simple fairy tale into a question about social responsibility and the cost of indifference."],"source":"Andersen, H. C. Andersen’s Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1597.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1597","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["water, air, and temperature make Earth distinctive","an image becomes more powerful than the person it follows","a girl’s hopeful images contrast with winter hardship","the Moon’s phases depend on sunlight and viewpoint"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["two young people searching for a mysterious bell","a young fir tree","the ocean, Moon, and Sun","a poor girl selling matches"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["the third planet from the Sun","a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve","coastlines around Earth","the Earth–Moon system"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["coastal water levels change in a repeating pattern","the child is cold and has not sold her matches","the scholar’s shadow gains power and challenges its former owner","people hear a distant sound but cannot agree where it comes from"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["she lights matches and imagines warmth, food, and her grandmother","he wears imaginary garments after advisers pretend to admire them","the article compares rocky inner planets with outer giants","the seekers move beyond familiar paths instead of trusting easy answers"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the tale ends tragically, exposing neglect of the poor","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","a child publicly says the emperor is wearing nothing","the guest reports an uncomfortable night and is accepted"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["each brief flame produces a comforting vision","the weavers claim foolish people cannot see their fabric","the test is based on an exaggerated sensitivity to a pea","the shadow speaks with confidence while the scholar knows the facts"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["curiosity may lead beyond convenient explanations","social pressure can make many people repeat a lie","compassion requires noticing suffering that others ignore","classification helps organize differences without erasing them"]},{"question":"In context, what does “vision” mean?","choices":["suitable for living things","an imagined or dreamlike sight","excessive pride in one’s appearance","light falling on an object"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["appearances and standards of status can be questioned","status should not replace truth as a standard for judgment","society has a duty to see people who are left outside","appreciating ordinary moments can prevent regret"]}],"answers":["C","D","B","B","A","A","A","C","B","C"]},{"id":"10-7","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":7,"genre":"Essay","title":"A Clue Is Not a Conclusion","paragraphs":["Detective fiction often rewards a reader who notices a detail before its importance becomes apparent. Yet a clue is not a conclusion. An object found near a crime may be relevant, accidental, or deliberately placed. Its meaning depends on context and on how well competing explanations survive scrutiny.","Stories about Sherlock Holmes dramatize this distinction. Holmes sometimes observes apparently ordinary features of clothing or behavior, but the strongest moments in his reasoning occur when he compares observations and tests an explanation against new facts. An impressive guess is not the same as a demonstrated inference. The writer of a mystery can control the sequence in which evidence appears; investigators in life cannot.","This difference should make readers careful. A narrator may invite confidence in a detective by emphasizing successful discoveries, while hiding unsuccessful hypotheses. We may enjoy that narrative arrangement without copying it uncritically. Evidence becomes powerful not because one detail seems dramatic, but because several independent details support a conclusion and plausible alternatives have been considered. Reading a mystery can thus become practice in disciplined doubt."],"source":"Original analytical essay referencing Conan Doyle, The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1661","questions":[{"question":"Which statement is the essay’s primary claim?","choices":["Rereading helps a reader notice reasons and supporting details","Patience involves persistent work rather than passive delay","A trustworthy inference considers context and alternatives","An experiment should distinguish a measured result from a universal claim"]},{"question":"Which example does the writer use to clarify the claim?","choices":["an object discovered near a crime","the example of a remembered fable","NASA’s DART collision with Dimorphos","a student evaluating a climate report"]},{"question":"Which difficulty or mistaken belief is discussed?","choices":["confusing disagreement with independent reasoning","the limits of attention during a first reading","the risk of treating one observation as proof","uncertainty about applying one test to every asteroid"]},{"question":"Which contrast is used to develop the argument?","choices":["shared agreement contrasted with independent observation","dramatic guessing contrasted with evidence-based reasoning","waiting without action versus purposeful persistence","reading once for events versus twice for interpretation"]},{"question":"Which supporting action or reasoning appears in the essay?","choices":["the tortoise keeps moving toward the finish","return to a passage when evidence is uncertain","a spacecraft strikes a moonlet to change orbital motion","the investigator compares observations with new facts"]},{"question":"What consequence or outcome does the writer identify?","choices":["an unsupported guess can become a checked explanation","multiple consistent details strengthen a conclusion","the result informs but does not settle planetary-defense questions","a reasoned position remains open to better evidence"]},{"question":"Which detail most directly supports the argument?","choices":["fictional detectives encounter evidence arranged by an author","the tortoise would not succeed by staying at the start","asteroids differ in composition, size, and trajectories","students first track unfamiliar words and names"]},{"question":"What inference follows from the writer’s reasoning?","choices":["consensus alone does not establish factual truth","comprehension improves when readers verify a claim","a plausible alternative must be tested rather than ignored","habits can be controlled more directly than outcomes"]},{"question":"Which phrase most accurately explains a key term from the essay?","choices":["scrutiny means careful and critical examination","kinetic impact means changing motion through a collision","investigation means a careful search for answers and evidence","autonomy means self-directed responsible judgment"]},{"question":"Which conclusion best respects the essay’s qualifications?","choices":["scientific confidence grows when conclusions include explicit limits","questioning a claim respectfully may protect a community from error","intellectual humility and critical independence can coexist","the best interpretation separates narrative excitement from reliable proof"]}],"answers":["C","A","C","B","D","B","A","C","A","D"]},{"id":"10-8","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":8,"genre":"News Report","title":"A Forecast for the Shore","paragraphs":["At a coastal market, fishers and small-boat operators compare the day's plans before leaving shore. One person wants to move a boat through a shallow channel. Another plans to walk across a tidal flat. Both activities depend partly on the sea's changing level. A path that looks wide and dry in the morning may be covered when the tide rises.","Tidal predictions are based on recurring astronomical influences, especially the gravitational effects of the Moon and Sun, together with measurements of local conditions. They tell users when high and low water are expected. The tidal range indicates how large the difference in height may be.","A forecast is not a substitute for observing the weather and local environment. Strong winds or unusual conditions can alter real water levels. Nevertheless, accurate tide information gives coastal communities a better starting point. The central lesson is practical: science becomes public knowledge when people use reliable evidence to make careful choices."],"source":"National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA). What Are Tides?","sourceUrl":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/tides.html","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["moons vary widely in structure and environment","tide knowledge helps people choose safer times","Dorothy begins a journey to find her way home","a tea party turns ordinary conversation into a puzzle"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["Mary Lennox, a lonely child","Tom Sawyer and his neighborhood friends","Dorothy and the Scarecrow","coastal residents using tidal information"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a wooden fence outside Aunt Polly’s home","a peculiar garden tea party","a large Yorkshire manor and its grounds","a busy coastal community"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["conversation does not follow the reasonable rules Alice expects","a cyclone carries Dorothy away from Kansas","a rising tide can surprise people who ignore local conditions","day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["he makes the work appear like a special privilege","she chooses to travel toward the Emerald City to seek advice","residents consult tidal predictions before planning activities","the article compares icy, volcanic, and atmosphere-rich satellites"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["better timing reduces avoidable exposure to coastal hazards","her journey begins with a goal and uncertainty about the path","the exchange exposes how words can confuse as well as communicate","other boys trade small treasures for a chance to paint"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the Scarecrow can speak thoughtfully despite doubting his intelligence","Titan has a thick atmosphere and Enceladus has a subsurface ocean","tidal range measures the difference between high and low water","Tom convinces others that painting requires unusual skill"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["curiosity can lead to discovery but also uncertainty","not every confident answer is logically sound","scientific information is most useful when joined with local judgment","new environments can make familiar behavior ineffective"]},{"question":"In context, what does “forecast” mean?","choices":["a statement that conflicts with another statement","a natural body orbiting another larger body","a prediction about conditions expected later","the act of influencing someone’s choice through words or example"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["planning requires translating evidence into decisions","discovery often begins with patient attention","actions can reveal abilities that labels deny","adaptability begins with recognizing changed circumstances"]}],"answers":["B","D","D","C","C","A","C","C","C","A"]},{"id":"10-9","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":9,"genre":"Poetry","title":"An Argument with the Sea","paragraphs":["The tide arrived with patient force\nAnd rubbed its questions on the stone.\nI called each breaking wave a cause;\nThe shore replied, “Not cause alone.”","The moon stood far beyond my sight,\nYet tugged the waters into change.\nThe wind wrote darker lines at night,\nIts local influence quick and strange.","I wanted one sufficient key,\nA single word to end debate.\nThe ocean showed complexity:\nSome answers come in pairs that wait.","At dawn I left the restless sand,\nNot certain I had conquered doubt.\nI knew more deeply where to stand:\nBegin with what the facts point out."],"source":"Original classroom poem using ocean-tide concepts from NOAA and the tradition of nature poetry; primary context NOAA","sourceUrl":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/tides.html","questions":[{"question":"What is the central idea expressed by the poem?","choices":["Small, steady progress offers a lesson for living","Representations are useful but cannot contain reality completely","Complex events often have multiple contributing causes","Knowledge grows through questioning rather than mere possession of books"]},{"question":"Which image is central to its meaning?","choices":["a changing sea and its shoreline","an atlas with boundaries and symbols","rain drawing lines on a window","a lantern between library shelves"]},{"question":"What is the main setting or situation in the poem?","choices":["the path and a flowing stream","a library during an evening reading session","a coast seen through night and dawn","a dim room looking toward a street at dusk"]},{"question":"Which change is experienced or observed by the speaker?","choices":["the speaker moves from busyness to reflection","the speaker first wants one simple explanation","the reader begins to check claims and dates","the speaker moves from certainty toward inquiry"]},{"question":"Which contrast or comparison shapes the imagery?","choices":["old light is compared with information from the past","rain and quiet become connected images","light represents partial guidance rather than complete knowledge","the moon and local wind are contrasted"]},{"question":"What emotional or intellectual movement does the poem show?","choices":["the reader gains a more qualified trust in models","the speaker accepts a more careful explanation","the creek continues after the speaker goes home","the reader leaves with notes and questions"]},{"question":"Which textual detail most clearly supports an interpretation?","choices":["waves and tide provide evidence of ongoing change","the water keeps finding a way through bends","the lantern illuminates only a small circle","boundaries look fixed although land and rivers change"]},{"question":"What might the central image or situation suggest?","choices":["different perspectives reveal different kinds of information","the stream suggests patient persistence","the ocean symbolizes the limits of simple answers","distant astronomy reveals limits of immediate perception"]},{"question":"Which option best explains one important word or expression?","choices":["sufficient means enough to explain or satisfy a need","convention means an agreed method of representing something","renewal means a sense of freshness or beginning again","claim means a statement offered as true"]},{"question":"How should the closing lines be understood?","choices":["the final color image suggests changed perception rather than magic","the poem supports precise maps while questioning claims of total certainty","the ending suggests that learning can continue beyond one walk","the final stanza favors evidence over false certainty"]}],"answers":["C","A","C","B","D","B","A","C","A","D"]},{"id":"10-10","grade":10,"color":"Ruby","num":10,"genre":"Science & Nonfiction","title":"Weather, Climate, and the Long View","paragraphs":["A thunderstorm can begin and end within a single afternoon. A season's usual temperature pattern takes much longer to describe. Meteorologists use the word weather for conditions at a particular place and time, including wind, rain, temperature, and clouds. Climate describes longer patterns calculated from many observations over years.","This difference matters when people interpret an unusual day. A cold morning does not disprove a long-term warming trend, just as one hot afternoon cannot establish a climate trend by itself. Scientists need records gathered from many places and times. They also examine how the atmosphere, oceans, and land influence each other.","Forecasts and climate projections answer related but distinct questions. A weather forecast asks what is likely to happen soon. A climate projection explores how distributions of conditions may change under stated assumptions. Neither kind of prediction is a promise without uncertainty. Readers should examine the timescale, evidence, and assumptions before comparing claims about tomorrow's weather with claims about decades of climate."],"source":"Original science explanation based on NOAA, What’s the Difference Between Climate and Weather?","sourceUrl":"https://www.noaa.gov/explainers/what-s-difference-between-climate-and-weather","questions":[{"question":"Which statement summarizes the principal scientific idea?","choices":["the Moon’s motion explains a common error in language","models simplify systems while preserving important relationships","weather describes short-term conditions while climate describes longer patterns","asteroids are diverse records of planetary history"]},{"question":"Which phenomenon or evidence is discussed?","choices":["wind, rain, temperature, and clouds","Earth rotating and orbiting the Sun","Earth’s Moon as observed by scientists","temperature, pressure, wind, and moisture data"]},{"question":"Which observations or methods are emphasized?","choices":["orbits within and beyond the main asteroid belt","the near and far sides of the Moon","many observations gathered across years","mathematical relationships and observed starting conditions"]},{"question":"What factor or complication is central to the explanation?","choices":["day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names","an unusual cold or hot day","the popular phrase dark side of the Moon gives a false impression","small rocky bodies are often mistaken for identical debris"]},{"question":"Which process or contrast does the text explain?","choices":["models and direct observations serve different roles","the essay compares rotation with revolution","the essay distinguishes a far side from a permanently unlit side","the difference between short forecasts and long projections"]},{"question":"What consequence or implication is described?","choices":["readers can explain the calendar’s occasional extra day","records allow researchers to describe long-term distributions","risk planning requires engineering and public deliberation","asteroids reveal information about the solar system’s formation"]},{"question":"Which observation gives evidence for the explanation?","choices":["one afternoon cannot establish a multidecade trend","different regions may be affected by a shared decision","many asteroids orbit between Mars and Jupiter","Earth takes about 365.25 days to complete one orbit"]},{"question":"What interpretation of the evidence is supported?","choices":["technical success does not replace ethical authorization","human calendars are models adjusted to natural cycles","different timescales require different kinds of evidence","familiar expressions can mislead when taken literally"]},{"question":"Which option best explains a key technical term?","choices":["projection means a possible future estimated under assumptions","happening at the same rate or in matching timing","one complete trip around another body","trajectory means the path followed by a moving body"]},{"question":"Which limitation or qualification does the text stress?","choices":["scientific models should expose assumptions and limitations","scientific terms should correct rather than reinforce misconceptions","accurate measurement sometimes requires practical adjustment","scientific claims should state timescale and uncertainty"]}],"answers":["C","A","C","B","D","B","A","C","A","D"]},{"id":"11-1","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":1,"genre":"Informational Passage","title":"A Moon Is Not Always Like Ours","paragraphs":["Earth's Moon is a rocky world with craters and almost no atmosphere. It is natural to imagine that other moons are similar. Space exploration has revealed a much wider variety. Some satellites are icy; some are heavily cratered; others show evidence of activity beneath their surfaces.","Saturn's moon Titan has a thick atmosphere and lakes of liquid hydrocarbons on its surface. Another Saturnian moon, Enceladus, has a hidden ocean beneath its icy exterior. Jupiter's moon Io is famous for intense volcanic activity. Small, irregular moons may be quite unlike the large, round Moon we see in our sky.","The word satellite describes a relationship—one object orbiting another—but it does not tell us everything about a world's materials or history. Scientists compare size, composition, motion, and surface evidence to learn how moons formed and changed. The comparisons also remind us that a familiar example should not become an unquestioned rule."],"source":"NASA. Moons: Facts.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["moons vary widely in structure and environment","Tom turns an unwanted chore into an object of desire","Dorothy begins a journey to find her way home","Alice follows a curious clue into an unfamiliar world"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["Dorothy, a girl far from home","natural satellites orbiting planets and other bodies","Earth rotating and orbiting the Sun","Dorothy and the Scarecrow"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a busy coastal community","a hallway flooded with Alice’s tears","different regions of the solar system","a cornfield beside the yellow brick road"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["Alice’s sudden changes in size create confusion and danger","the familiar image of Earth’s Moon does not fit every moon","conversation does not follow the reasonable rules Alice expects","a rising tide can surprise people who ignore local conditions"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["Dorothy invites him to travel to the Wizard","he makes the work appear like a special privilege","the article compares icy, volcanic, and atmosphere-rich satellites","Alice asks questions and challenges contradictory statements"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["other boys trade small treasures for a chance to paint","he joins the journey and begins showing practical judgment","readers see moons as diverse worlds rather than identical companions","better timing reduces avoidable exposure to coastal hazards"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["tears shed while she was enormous later form a pool","the yellow brick road offers direction but not a guarantee of safety","Titan has a thick atmosphere and Enceladus has a subsurface ocean","Tom convinces others that painting requires unusual skill"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["scientific categories contain meaningful internal differences","self-doubt may hide an ability already being used","not every confident answer is logically sound","places can hold memories as well as physical objects"]},{"question":"In context, what does “satellite” mean?","choices":["kept apart from other people or places","a natural body orbiting another larger body","the place someone hopes to reach","one complete trip around another body"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["comparison can correct assumptions created by one example","critical reading includes examining how language is used","actions can reveal abilities that labels deny","having a direction is different from knowing every answer"]}],"answers":["A","B","C","B","C","C","C","A","B","A"]},{"id":"11-2","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":2,"genre":"Fable","title":"The Fox and the Grapes","paragraphs":["A hungry fox wandered beside a vineyard and noticed a bunch of ripe grapes hanging from a high vine. The fruit looked juicy and sweet. He stood beneath it, leaped into the air, and missed by a small distance. He tried again with a longer run. This time his paws brushed a leaf, but he still could not reach the grapes.","The fox rested for a moment and counted his chances. He moved a stone, stood upon it, and stretched his neck. The bunch remained too high. After several more unsuccessful jumps, he began to feel foolish and tired.","As he walked away, he announced that the grapes were probably sour anyway. Yet nothing had changed about the fruit. Only the fox's hope of eating it had disappeared. His final comment protected his pride, but it did not tell the truth about the grapes."],"source":"Aesop. Aesop’s Fables (V. S. Vernon Jones, trans.). Project Gutenberg, eBook 11339.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["a dog loses a real meal while chasing an illusion","a fox changes his opinion after failing to reach fruit","a farmer sacrifices steady wealth for a quick gain","a bird uses pebbles to reach drinking water"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["a thirsty crow","a farmer and a remarkable goose","a dog carrying a piece of meat","a hungry fox"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a meadow changing from summer to winter","a woodland","a vineyard wall","a hot courtyard"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["water lies too low inside a narrow pitcher","the farmer wants all the gold at once","the shepherd repeatedly calls for help without danger","grapes hang beyond the fox’s reach"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["he destroys the goose hoping to find more gold inside","the fox jumps repeatedly and then walks away","the crow drops small stones into the pitcher","the wind blows hard but the sun shines gently"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the fox pretends the grapes were not worth eating","winter finds the ants prepared and the grasshopper hungry","the tortoise reaches the finish first","no one believes him when a wolf truly appears"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the image in the stream is only a reflection","the bunch remains untouched above his head","the hare falls asleep before completing the race","the ants work when food is plentiful"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["planning protects people when conditions change","productive effort is the true source of lasting wealth","gentle influence can succeed where force fails","people may criticize what they cannot obtain"]},{"question":"In context, what does “sour” mean?","choices":["able to use available things cleverly","sharp or unpleasant in taste","a warning that something dangerous is happening","speak too proudly about an ability"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["dishonesty harms both the speaker and others","kindness can be more effective than pressure","contentment guards against needless loss","disappointment can tempt people to make excuses"]}],"answers":["B","D","C","D","B","A","B","D","B","D"]},{"id":"11-3","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":3,"genre":"Fiction","title":"The White Snake","paragraphs":["A servant at a king's court once discovered a curious secret: after tasting a special white snake, he could understand the speech of animals. When trouble later forced him to leave the palace, he traveled through the countryside and began to listen carefully.","He saved fish stranded on a bank, protected ants from being trampled, and spared young ravens. None of these creatures could reward him immediately. Eventually the servant hoped to marry a princess, but her father demanded tasks that seemed impossible. The young man had to recover a ring from deep water and gather countless grains scattered in grass.","The fish returned the ring, and the ants collected the grain. Other animals helped him face further tests. His ability to understand them mattered, but his earlier kindness mattered more. He had been willing to help creatures that seemed powerless. When he finally needed assistance, they remembered him."],"source":"Grimm, J., & Grimm, W. Grimms’ Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 2591.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2591","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["animals repay the prince who defended them","a servant succeeds with aid from creatures he spared","secret help leads to grateful giving","a traveler solves the mystery of worn-out shoes"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["twelve princesses and a watchful traveler","a princess and an enchanted frog","a servant who understands animals","a gentle youngest prince and his older brothers"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a forest and a royal town","a forest path","a family dining room","a kingdom of fields, rivers, and forests"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["a princess is captured and no single brother can rescue her","the servant must complete impossible tasks to win a princess","the shoemaker has leather for only one final pair","the princess loses a golden ball and makes a promise she regrets"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["the traveler stays awake and follows the princesses invisibly","he shares food with an old man and receives a golden goose","he assists animals and later asks for their help","the brothers combine searching, stealing, shooting, and tailoring skills"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the enchanted castle is released through the animals’ help","they save the princess through teamwork","the animals help recover a ring and gather scattered grain","the cat survives while the fox cannot choose a trick quickly enough"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the mysterious shoes are sewn with extraordinary skill","branches of silver and gold mark their hidden route","anyone trying to grab the goose becomes stuck to it","the servant gains his unusual gift after tasting a white snake"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["one useful skill can be better than many empty claims","careful observation can uncover an unlikely explanation","promises reveal character when keeping them is inconvenient","helping others creates relationships that can solve problems"]},{"question":"In context, what does “impossible” mean?","choices":["too eager to praise one’s own abilities","the respect owed to every person","trained for one particular kind of work","seemingly unable to be done"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["respect for others can matter more than clever appearances","compassion is linked to clever problem-solving","shared success need not belong to one person","generosity can create a circle of goodwill"]}],"answers":["B","C","D","B","C","C","D","D","D","B"]},{"id":"11-4","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":4,"genre":"Bible Scripture","title":"Ecclesiastes: Seasons, Work, and Human Limits","paragraphs":["The book of Ecclesiastes examines the search for a meaningful life. Its speaker notices that human beings plan, labor, celebrate, and grieve, yet do not control the passage of time. The familiar reflection about a season for different experiences places opposites side by side: building and breaking down, speaking and keeping silent, mourning and dancing. These pairs do not suggest that every action is equally wise. They underline how circumstances alter what is fitting.","The writer also observes that work may bring satisfaction while refusing to guarantee permanent security. Wealth, reputation, and effort cannot cancel the uncertainty of life. Such observations may initially sound pessimistic, but the text can also be read as a criticism of exaggerated human confidence. By acknowledging limits, a person may learn humility and appreciate ordinary moments.","A thoughtful reader should distinguish description from instruction. When the speaker says that varied events have their seasons, the passage describes the breadth of human experience; it does not command every event to occur. Its wisdom comes partly from holding two ideas in tension: responsible action matters, yet outcomes are never fully controlled by human intention."],"source":"Ecclesiastes 3:1–13 and related reflections; original classroom interpretive paraphrase (KJV)","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best captures the central teaching of this scripture-based reading?","choices":["Responsible courage may involve calculated personal risk","Effective argument may begin with shared evidence and then challenge assumptions","Loyalty can require choosing companionship despite uncertainty","Wisdom includes recognizing both meaningful action and human limits"]},{"question":"Which person or group is most central to the account?","choices":["Ruth and her mother-in-law Naomi","the reflective speaker of Ecclesiastes","David, the young shepherd","the Samaritan who helped the traveler"]},{"question":"What context or event establishes the situation?","choices":["Naomi prepares to return to Bethlehem","the road from Jerusalem to Jericho","a city filled with public debate and religious images","a sequence of contrasting seasons in human life"]},{"question":"Which tension or problem drives the account?","choices":["approaching the king uninvited is dangerous","soldiers are intimidated by a heavily armed opponent","people cannot command every outcome through effort","a traveler is hurt and abandoned by robbers"]},{"question":"Which important action or decision is described?","choices":["the speaker sets paired experiences beside one another","David selects a sling and smooth stones","Paul refers to an altar dedicated to an unknown god","Ruth chooses to travel with Naomi"]},{"question":"What result or response follows?","choices":["the two women arrive together in Bethlehem","David defeats the warrior in the confrontation","the reflection encourages humility and appreciation","listeners react with mockery, curiosity, or belief"]},{"question":"Which detail gives specific support to the account?","choices":["Esther asks her community to fast before she acts","work brings satisfaction but not permanent certainty","an existing inscription becomes a point of contact","two earlier travelers pass without helping"]},{"question":"What inference about motive or meaning is most justified?","choices":["persuasion requires attention to an audience without promising agreement","being a neighbor is shown through actions rather than labels","Ruth values belonging and faithfulness more than a promised reward","describing life’s changes is not endorsing every possible act"]},{"question":"Which phrase best explains a significant word or idea in the passage?","choices":["commitment means a lasting promise shown in conduct","pessimistic means emphasizing discouraging aspects or limits","mercy means compassionate help toward someone suffering","rhetorical means concerned with persuasive communication"]},{"question":"Which broader interpretation is most consistent with the passage?","choices":["the apparent opposites create a nuanced rather than simple lesson","the narrative connects faith and preparation rather than treating courage as noise","the narrative links influence to an obligation toward vulnerable people","the story respects grief while showing the strength of solidarity"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"11-5","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":5,"genre":"Novel","title":"A Mysterious Reef at Sea","paragraphs":["In the nineteenth century, vessels crossing distant oceans began reporting a strange object beneath the waves. Some captains described a gigantic creature that moved with impossible speed. Others believed it had damaged the hulls of powerful ships. Newspapers carried alarming stories, and fear grew because the sightings seemed to come from different parts of the world.","Professor Pierre Aronnax, a scientist interested in marine life, considered possible explanations. Could an unknown animal be responsible? Was the evidence exaggerated? The uncertainty was especially troubling because reports often mixed observation with speculation. People who had never seen the object repeated dramatic accounts as though they were proven facts.","Aronnax accepted an invitation to join an expedition searching for the creature. His decision placed him at the intersection of scientific curiosity and public excitement. In Jules Verne's novel, the investigation becomes more complex than anyone expects: the suspected monster is connected to a technological secret. The opening episode asks readers to notice how a hypothesis develops—and why a convincing story is not the same thing as confirmed evidence."],"source":"Verne, J. Twenty Thousand Leagues under the Sea. Project Gutenberg, eBook 164.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/164","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["asteroids are diverse records of planetary history","sailors seek the truth about a supposed sea monster","an intentional spacecraft impact tests a defense idea","the contrast among planets shows both the value and limit of labels"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["Professor Aronnax and an international expedition","Earth’s Moon as observed by scientists","planetary-defense scientists","Professor Aronnax, Ned Land, Conseil, and Captain Nemo"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a submerged landscape","the open ocean in the nineteenth century","orbits within and beyond the main asteroid belt","the near and far sides of the Moon"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["the beauty of discovery is mixed with the danger of captivity","the popular phrase dark side of the Moon gives a false impression","simple planet labels can hide major physical differences","ships report damage attributed to a mysterious marine creature"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["researchers compare compositions, paths, and impact records","Aronnax joins a voyage to investigate the reports","Nemo displays knowledge of ocean life while restricting his guests","DART deliberately strikes the moonlet Dimorphos in September 2022"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the search raises doubts about the first explanation","asteroids reveal information about the solar system’s formation","the seafloor reveals unexpected natural beauty and danger","Aronnax admires the science but worries about their lack of choice"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["witnesses describe something that moves with surprising speed","he continues to ask for brains even while solving problems","Nemo’s independence depends on living apart from ordinary society","the inner planets are rocky while the outer four are giant planets"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["extraordinary claims should be tested against better evidence","classification can reveal a history concealed by appearance","useful categories are tools rather than final explanations","controlled experiments can inform future safety strategies"]},{"question":"In context, what does “hypothesis” mean?","choices":["the way a person thinks about their own character","happening at the same rate or in matching timing","a proposed explanation that can be investigated","a system for classifying things into related groups"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["investigation can overturn a widely accepted story","progress should be considered alongside ethical responsibility","scientific understanding changes when stronger evidence appears","preparation is more reliable when methods are tested beforehand"]}],"answers":["B","A","B","D","B","A","A","A","C","A"]},{"id":"11-6","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":6,"genre":"Short Story","title":"The Shadow That Wanted to Rule","paragraphs":["A thoughtful scholar once lived in a warm country and spent much of his time reading and writing. One evening his shadow separated from him and began an independent life. Years passed before it returned, now dressed richly and speaking like a person of influence. The scholar was surprised that the shadow seemed so successful.","The shadow had learned to listen to secrets and use them for advantage. It claimed to understand the world better than its former master. Soon it asked the scholar to travel as its companion, pretending that the scholar was the servant. In public, fine clothing and confident speech persuaded people to admire the shadow.","The story turns an ordinary fact of light into an unsettling symbol. A shadow should depend on the person who casts it, yet here the image takes control. Andersen challenges readers to consider why societies sometimes trust powerful appearances more than honest knowledge. The fiction feels magical, but its warning about reputation and manipulation remains recognizable. The shadow could manipulate public opinion because it understood the power of appearances."],"source":"Andersen, H. C. Andersen’s Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1597.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1597","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["water, air, and temperature make Earth distinctive","an image becomes more powerful than the person it follows","a strange bedroom test determines royal identity","a distant bell invites a search for deeper meaning"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["two young people searching for a mysterious bell","a poor girl selling matches","a scholar and his independent shadow","a young fir tree"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a forest beyond a busy town","a freezing city street on New Year’s Eve","coastlines around Earth","a city where appearances influence reputation"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["readers must explain why Earth supports familiar forms of life","coastal water levels change in a repeating pattern","the tree is unhappy with its present life and longs for the future","the scholar’s shadow gains power and challenges its former owner"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the shadow dresses as an important man and manipulates social opinion","the seekers move beyond familiar paths instead of trusting easy answers","it waits eagerly to grow tall and become a Christmas tree","she hides a tiny pea beneath many mattresses"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["planetary diversity becomes a tool for learning how worlds form","society rewards the convincing shadow rather than the truthful scholar","moon phases are understood as patterns of illumination","after the celebration it is forgotten and its earlier happiness is lost"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the sound seems different from each crowded street","the tree barely notices sunshine or birds while imagining later glory","liquid water covers roughly seventy-one percent of Earth’s surface","the shadow speaks with confidence while the scholar knows the facts"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["a polished appearance can disguise a lack of integrity","constant longing may hide the value of the present","compassion requires noticing suffering that others ignore","habitable environments depend on several conditions working together"]},{"question":"In context, what does “manipulate” mean?","choices":["control others unfairly through clever influence","an imagined or dreamlike sight","excessive pride in one’s appearance","difficult to explain or understand"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["status should not replace truth as a standard for judgment","understanding may demand patient exploration","similar origins can produce very different environments","knowing natural patterns improves safety and planning"]}],"answers":["B","C","D","D","A","B","D","A","A","A"]},{"id":"11-7","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":7,"genre":"Essay","title":"When Experiments Change Our Questions","paragraphs":["Scientific experiments are often described as methods for confirming ideas. Their deeper value may be that they make a question more precise. NASA's DART spacecraft deliberately struck the asteroid moonlet Dimorphos in 2022 to test whether a collision could alter an orbit. The project did not prove that every dangerous asteroid can be redirected. It provided evidence about one particular technique under particular conditions.","A careful discussion of the mission must separate its objective, observation, and interpretation. The objective was to test kinetic impact as a method of changing motion. The observation concerned a measurable orbital change. The interpretation asks how far that result can be applied to other objects, whose size, composition, and trajectories may differ.","This distinction matters beyond astronomy. In health, education, and environmental studies, a result should be useful without being exaggerated. One successful case is evidence, not a universal rule. Good experimental thinking therefore welcomes both accomplishment and limitation: the same data that support a conclusion can reveal what researchers still need to test."],"source":"Original scientific essay using NASA, Double Asteroid Redirection Test mission","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dart/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement is the essay’s primary claim?","choices":["Independent judgment needs evidence and willingness to revise","Rereading helps a reader notice reasons and supporting details","Social pressure can make obvious errors seem acceptable","An experiment should distinguish a measured result from a universal claim"]},{"question":"Which example does the writer use to clarify the claim?","choices":["a student evaluating a climate report","NASA’s DART collision with Dimorphos","the gardener and student examples","the example of a remembered fable"]},{"question":"Which difficulty or mistaken belief is discussed?","choices":["the expectation that results should arrive immediately","fear of being judged foolish","the risk of treating one observation as proof","uncertainty about applying one test to every asteroid"]},{"question":"Which contrast is used to develop the argument?","choices":["reading once for events versus twice for interpretation","dramatic guessing contrasted with evidence-based reasoning","objectives and observations contrasted with interpretation","shared agreement contrasted with independent observation"]},{"question":"Which supporting action or reasoning appears in the essay?","choices":["a spacecraft strikes a moonlet to change orbital motion","return to a passage when evidence is uncertain","the reader evaluates observations, uncertainty, and alternatives","a child states what others refuse to acknowledge"]},{"question":"What consequence or outcome does the writer identify?","choices":["honest verification can interrupt a public illusion","multiple consistent details strengthen a conclusion","the result informs but does not settle planetary-defense questions","a reasoned position remains open to better evidence"]},{"question":"Which detail most directly supports the argument?","choices":["people repeat claims without checking sources","asteroids differ in composition, size, and trajectories","students first track unfamiliar words and names","a famous institution is not itself a complete proof"]},{"question":"What inference follows from the writer’s reasoning?","choices":["comprehension improves when readers verify a claim","stronger claims ordinarily demand stronger supporting evidence","habits can be controlled more directly than outcomes","one successful demonstration does not guarantee all future outcomes"]},{"question":"Which phrase most accurately explains a key term from the essay?","choices":["investigation means a careful search for answers and evidence","kinetic impact means changing motion through a collision","restraint means controlling an impulse or reaction","autonomy means self-directed responsible judgment"]},{"question":"Which conclusion best respects the essay’s qualifications?","choices":["scientific confidence grows when conclusions include explicit limits","the best interpretation separates narrative excitement from reliable proof","rereading is a targeted strategy rather than a sign of failure","questioning a claim respectfully may protect a community from error"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"11-8","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":8,"genre":"News Report","title":"Watching for a Space Rock","paragraphs":["A telescope detects a faint moving point against distant stars. At first, astronomers know little about the object except that its position changes over time. Additional observations allow them to estimate its orbit. The object may be an asteroid or a comet, and the crucial question is whether its path could bring it close to Earth.","NASA's planetary-defense work coordinates efforts to discover, track, and characterize near-Earth objects. Scientists compare observations gathered on different nights and may refine their estimates as new measurements arrive. A headline that calls an object 'dangerous' without giving its estimated path or uncertainty can create unnecessary alarm.","Planetary defense is not only a story about collisions. It is also a demonstration of how scientists handle incomplete information. A risk estimate is revised when better evidence becomes available, and a close approach does not automatically mean an impact. Reliable public communication must explain both what observers know and what remains uncertain. Careful monitoring makes a more useful response possible than either panic or careless reassurance. A revised trajectory can change the estimated likelihood of an encounter."],"source":"NASA. Asteroid Facts.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["the contrast among planets shows both the value and limit of labels","systematic observation supports asteroid-risk decisions","an underwater journey changes the scientist’s sense of nature","the Moon’s motion explains a common error in language"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["Professor Aronnax, Ned Land, Conseil, and Captain Nemo","planetary scientists comparing worlds","planetary-defense scientists","asteroids and the scientists who study them"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["the long road to the Emerald City","the near and far sides of the Moon","observatories on Earth and in space","a remarkable submarine beneath the ocean"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["scientists need evidence that a spacecraft could alter an asteroid’s movement","simple planet labels can hide major physical differences","some near-Earth objects could eventually pose an impact hazard","the popular phrase dark side of the Moon gives a false impression"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["Nemo displays knowledge of ocean life while restricting his guests","his companions notice his useful suggestions during difficulties","scientists locate, track, and refine the predicted paths of objects","the essay distinguishes a far side from a permanently unlit side"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["classification helps inquiry but must be revised when needed","the Moon’s rotation and orbit explain our stable viewing angle","the supposed sea monster is revealed as the submarine Nautilus","monitoring helps officials distinguish low risks from meaningful threats"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["the journey depends on equipment that provides breathing support","NASA coordinates planetary-defense observations and warnings","Nemo’s independence depends on living apart from ordinary society","he continues to ask for brains even while solving problems"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["classification can reveal a history concealed by appearance","useful categories are tools rather than final explanations","risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured","a new observation can force the revision of an earlier conclusion"]},{"question":"In context, what does “trajectory” mean?","choices":["the materials from which something is made","completely surrounded or covered by liquid","happening at the same rate or in matching timing","the path followed by a moving object"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["competence is better assessed by evidence than by a label","scientific terms should correct rather than reinforce misconceptions","scientific categories should guide questions rather than prevent them","credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear"]}],"answers":["B","C","C","C","C","D","B","C","D","D"]},{"id":"11-9","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":9,"genre":"Poetry","title":"The Observatory at Midnight","paragraphs":["Midnight gathers on the dome;\nA telescope receives old fire.\nIts mirror makes no star its own,\nYet turns faint signals into inquiry.","I used to name the sky a map,\nA finished diagram of places;\nNow distant light disrupts the chart\nAnd carries time between the spaces.","The galaxy is not a jewel\nArranged for one observer's praise.\nIts ancient brightness bears a trace\nOf worlds we meet through delayed rays.","I write one figure in my book,\nThen put a question at its side.\nMeasurement is not a lock\nThat keeps new meanings sealed inside.","Before the eastern windows pale,\nI leave the dome with less to claim;\nAnd more to seek within the dark\nThan certainty had ever named."],"source":"Original classroom poem about observation and astronomy; factual context NASA Webb first images","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/webbs-first-images/","questions":[{"question":"What is the central idea expressed by the poem?","choices":["Complex events often have multiple contributing causes","Knowledge grows through questioning rather than mere possession of books","Small, steady progress offers a lesson for living","Observation can increase questions as well as knowledge"]},{"question":"Which image is central to its meaning?","choices":["a creek moving beside a gate","a telescope receiving faint distant light","a changing sea and its shoreline","an atlas with boundaries and symbols"]},{"question":"What is the main setting or situation in the poem?","choices":["a coast seen through night and dawn","a dim room looking toward a street at dusk","a library during an evening reading session","an observatory at midnight"]},{"question":"Which change is experienced or observed by the speaker?","choices":["a leaf moves while a pebble stays still","the reader begins to check claims and dates","the speaker moves from certainty toward inquiry","the speaker moves from busyness to reflection"]},{"question":"Which contrast or comparison shapes the imagery?","choices":["old light is compared with information from the past","the moon and local wind are contrasted","the speaker takes steps beside the water","light represents partial guidance rather than complete knowledge"]},{"question":"What emotional or intellectual movement does the poem show?","choices":["the speaker accepts a more careful explanation","the morning appears refreshed","measurement opens rather than closes interpretation","the reader leaves with notes and questions"]},{"question":"Which textual detail most clearly supports an interpretation?","choices":["boundaries look fixed although land and rivers change","the speaker writes a figure and also a question","waves and tide provide evidence of ongoing change","the lantern illuminates only a small circle"]},{"question":"What might the central image or situation suggest?","choices":["different perspectives reveal different kinds of information","a text is useful when the reader examines it independently","the ocean symbolizes the limits of simple answers","distant astronomy reveals limits of immediate perception"]},{"question":"Which option best explains one important word or expression?","choices":["renewal means a sense of freshness or beginning again","inquiry means purposeful questioning or investigation","sufficient means enough to explain or satisfy a need","convention means an agreed method of representing something"]},{"question":"How should the closing lines be understood?","choices":["the final stanza treats uncertainty as intellectual opportunity","the final color image suggests changed perception rather than magic","the ending values careful thinking more than collecting facts","the ending suggests that learning can continue beyond one walk"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"11-10","grade":11,"color":"Amethyst","num":10,"genre":"Science & Nonfiction","title":"Models, Measurements, and Uncertainty","paragraphs":["To study a complicated system, scientists often build a model: a simplified representation that preserves relationships important to a question. Weather researchers combine observations of temperature, pressure, wind, and moisture with equations describing atmospheric processes. Computers then estimate how conditions may change. A model is neither a miniature copy of the planet nor an infallible prediction.","Different models and starting observations may produce different results. Instead of hiding this variation, researchers can use it to estimate uncertainty. Short-term forecasts tend to become less specific as the time horizon extends because small differences in initial conditions can grow. Long-term climate work presents a different challenge: representing broad patterns and changes under stated assumptions.","A responsible reader should ask what the model was built to explain, what measurements informed it, and which limitations remain. Precision in a number does not automatically mean certainty in a conclusion. The strength of scientific modeling lies in making assumptions visible and comparing predictions with observations, not in claiming that complex systems have become perfectly predictable."],"source":"Original science explainer informed by NOAA weather and climate explanation","sourceUrl":"https://www.noaa.gov/explainers/what-s-difference-between-climate-and-weather","questions":[{"question":"Which statement summarizes the principal scientific idea?","choices":["weather describes short-term conditions while climate describes longer patterns","the calendar reflects Earth’s two different motions","asteroids are diverse records of planetary history","models simplify systems while preserving important relationships"]},{"question":"Which phenomenon or evidence is discussed?","choices":["Earth’s Moon as observed by scientists","temperature, pressure, wind, and moisture data","asteroids and the scientists who study them","the DART collision with an asteroid moonlet"]},{"question":"Which observations or methods are emphasized?","choices":["orbits within and beyond the main asteroid belt","trajectory, composition, size, and available response time","Earth’s path through space","mathematical relationships and observed starting conditions"]},{"question":"What factor or complication is central to the explanation?","choices":["day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names","small rocky bodies are often mistaken for identical debris","uncertainty grows as forecast horizons extend","possible uncertainty and unintended consequences"]},{"question":"Which process or contrast does the text explain?","choices":["models and direct observations serve different roles","the essay distinguishes a far side from a permanently unlit side","the essay compares rotation with revolution","mission data inform but do not settle every future decision"]},{"question":"What consequence or implication is described?","choices":["risk planning requires engineering and public deliberation","the Moon’s rotation and orbit explain our stable viewing angle","comparing predictions with data helps evaluate models","readers can explain the calendar’s occasional extra day"]},{"question":"Which observation gives evidence for the explanation?","choices":["one afternoon cannot establish a multidecade trend","small differences in initial conditions may amplify","many asteroids orbit between Mars and Jupiter","different regions may be affected by a shared decision"]},{"question":"What interpretation of the evidence is supported?","choices":["different timescales require different kinds of evidence","human calendars are models adjusted to natural cycles","technical success does not replace ethical authorization","a precise-looking number is not automatically a certain result"]},{"question":"Which option best explains a key technical term?","choices":["happening at the same rate or in matching timing","infallible means incapable of making mistakes","trajectory means the path followed by a moving body","projection means a possible future estimated under assumptions"]},{"question":"Which limitation or qualification does the text stress?","choices":["scientific models should expose assumptions and limitations","accurate measurement sometimes requires practical adjustment","responsible choices require both workable methods and transparent uncertainty","scientific terms should correct rather than reinforce misconceptions"]}],"answers":["D","B","D","C","A","C","B","D","B","A"]},{"id":"12-1","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":1,"genre":"Informational Passage","title":"Planetary Categories and Their Limits","paragraphs":["It is convenient to divide our solar system into two broad neighborhoods. Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars are small rocky planets close to the Sun. Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune are much larger worlds farther away. Such a classification quickly communicates an important pattern, but it can also become an obstacle if readers assume that all members of a category are almost identical.","Venus and Earth are both rocky, yet the surface of Venus is extremely hot and its atmosphere is radically different. Saturn and Jupiter are both gas giants, but their internal properties, surrounding moons, and rings are not identical. Uranus and Neptune are often grouped as ice giants because their compositions differ from those of Jupiter and Saturn. No single label can describe every scientifically important characteristic.","The point is not to abandon classification. Without it, comparison would be more difficult. Instead, good scientific reasoning treats categories as provisional tools built to answer specific questions. Astronomers can ask why two planets share a feature and why they differ in another. This combination of grouping and close observation is central to evidence-based explanation. A useful taxonomy should help scientists frame questions, not end investigation."],"source":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA). About the Planets.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["the submarine offers learning and confinement together","an underwater journey changes the scientist’s sense of nature","the contrast among planets shows both the value and limit of labels","sailors seek the truth about a supposed sea monster"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["Earth’s Moon as observed by scientists","Professor Aronnax, Ned Land, Conseil, and Captain Nemo","Captain Nemo and Professor Aronnax","planetary scientists comparing worlds"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["the libraries and observation rooms of the Nautilus","the long road to the Emerald City","the inner and outer solar system","orbits within and beyond the main asteroid belt"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["some near-Earth objects could eventually pose an impact hazard","the beauty of discovery is mixed with the danger of captivity","scientists need evidence that a spacecraft could alter an asteroid’s movement","simple planet labels can hide major physical differences"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the explorers use diving suits and follow their guide","scientists locate, track, and refine the predicted paths of objects","researchers compare materials, atmospheres, and orbits across planetary groups","they board a vessel built and commanded by Captain Nemo"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["measurements show that the impact changed the moonlet’s orbit","classification helps inquiry but must be revised when needed","the search raises doubts about the first explanation","the supposed sea monster is revealed as the submarine Nautilus"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the journey depends on equipment that provides breathing support","he continues to ask for brains even while solving problems","many asteroids orbit between Mars and Jupiter","the inner planets are rocky while the outer four are giant planets"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["identity claims need to be weighed against observable behavior","a new observation can force the revision of an earlier conclusion","risk assessment improves when uncertainties are measured","useful categories are tools rather than final explanations"]},{"question":"In context, what does “taxonomy” mean?","choices":["a system for classifying things into related groups","the discovery of something previously hidden","the way a person thinks about their own character","happening at the same rate or in matching timing"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["progress should be considered alongside ethical responsibility","credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear","small objects can preserve important evidence about the past","scientific categories should guide questions rather than prevent them"]}],"answers":["C","D","C","D","C","B","D","D","A","D"]},{"id":"12-2","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":2,"genre":"Fable","title":"The Farmer and His Sons","paragraphs":["An elderly farmer worried that his sons would neglect the vineyard when he was gone. They disliked digging, weeding, and caring for the vines. Before his death, he told them that a treasure was hidden somewhere among the rows of grapes. He did not say exactly where it lay.","After the farmer died, the sons gathered their tools and dug carefully through the entire field. They turned over the soil near each vine, expecting to strike a metal chest. No coins or jewels appeared. At first they felt disappointed.","But when the growing season ended, the vineyard produced its finest harvest in years. The sons realized that their digging had loosened the soil and helped the vines grow. They sold the grapes and earned more than before. Their father's treasure was not a box in the earth. It was the reward of work they had finally learned to do. Their digging helped cultivate the vines rather than uncover a box."],"source":"Aesop. Aesop’s Fables (V. S. Vernon Jones, trans.). Project Gutenberg, eBook 11339.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11339","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["a father teaches the value of labor through a search","a small creature repays a powerful one","the sun wins a contest by giving warmth","false warnings make a real emergency harder to face"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["a dog carrying a piece of meat","a patient tortoise and a speedy hare","a young shepherd","an elderly farmer and his sons"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a meadow changing from summer to winter","a hot courtyard","a family vineyard","a road beneath the sky"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the sons avoid the hard work needed to maintain the vines","the farmer wants all the gold at once","water lies too low inside a narrow pitcher","two forces argue about which is stronger"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the tortoise walks steadily while the hare naps","they dig the entire vineyard searching for buried treasure","the dog opens its mouth to seize the imagined second piece","the wind blows hard but the sun shines gently"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the well-cultivated soil produces a rich harvest","the trapped lion gains freedom","winter finds the ants prepared and the grasshopper hungry","the water rises to the bird’s beak"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["the hare falls asleep before completing the race","the goose had produced one golden egg each day","his earlier false alarms damage his credibility","the father mentions treasure hidden among the vines"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["careful thinking can solve a practical problem","planning protects people when conditions change","people may criticize what they cannot obtain","productive effort is the true source of lasting wealth"]},{"question":"In context, what does “cultivate” mean?","choices":["able to use available things cleverly","make ready for something that will happen","an excessive desire for more than one needs","work the soil so plants can grow well"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["ingenuity can achieve what force cannot","kindness can return from an unexpected source","work can be more valuable than a hidden fortune","disappointment can tempt people to make excuses"]}],"answers":["A","D","C","A","B","A","D","D","D","C"]},{"id":"12-3","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":3,"genre":"Fiction","title":"The Four Clever Brothers","paragraphs":["Four brothers left home to learn different trades. One became an expert at seeing distant things, another learned to take objects without being noticed, a third became a precise marksman, and the last mastered extraordinary sewing. Years later they met again and compared what they could do.","They heard that a dragon had carried away a princess. The first brother looked through his special glass and found the dragon's hiding place. The second helped remove the princess from danger. The third defended the group when the monster attacked. Finally, when their boat was damaged, the fourth used his skill to repair it.","The princess reached safety because every brother completed a part of the plan. Each had once thought his own trade was the most important. Yet the adventure made it difficult to name a single hero. Their skills had not become identical; instead, their differences formed a stronger team. Their specialized skills became stronger when combined in a shared plan."],"source":"Grimm, J., & Grimm, W. Grimms’ Fairy Tales. Project Gutenberg, eBook 2591.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2591","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["four unwanted animals find safety as a team","a rescue succeeds because four talents fit together","secret help leads to grateful giving","a traveler solves the mystery of worn-out shoes"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["a cat and a boastful fox","a kind younger brother called Simpleton","four brothers with different expert skills","a poor shoemaker and two helpful elves"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["a small shoe workshop","a kingdom threatened by a dragon","a family dining room","a forest and an enchanted castle"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["the brothers threaten small animals and cannot solve castle tasks","the family treats an aging grandfather without dignity","the shoemaker has leather for only one final pair","a princess is captured and no single brother can rescue her"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["the traveler stays awake and follows the princesses invisibly","the brothers combine searching, stealing, shooting, and tailoring skills","he assists animals and later asks for their help","the cat climbs a tree when hunting dogs arrive"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the parents recognize their cruelty and change their behavior","he reveals the secret midnight dances","they save the princess through teamwork","the enchanted castle is released through the animals’ help"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["their combined cries sound terrifying in the darkness","each brother contributes a different ability to the rescue","the princess initially refuses to welcome her unlikely helper","anyone trying to grab the goose becomes stuck to it"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["specialized talents become powerful when coordinated","care for vulnerable lives may bring unexpected allies","promises reveal character when keeping them is inconvenient","kindness may open doors that pride keeps closed"]},{"question":"In context, what does “specialized” mean?","choices":["work together toward a shared goal","skill in making things carefully","a responsibility created by a promise or duty","trained for one particular kind of work"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["respect for others can matter more than clever appearances","shared success need not belong to one person","friendship gives courage to those who feel discarded","gratitude requires action rather than pleasant words"]}],"answers":["B","C","B","D","B","C","B","A","D","B"]},{"id":"12-4","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":4,"genre":"Bible Scripture","title":"Paul at Athens: Argument in a Public Square","paragraphs":["When Paul arrived in Athens, he encountered a city marked by philosophical debate and religious images. In its public spaces, people exchanged arguments about the world and human purpose. Paul spoke with different groups, and some listeners invited him to explain his ideas before a council associated with public discussion.","Rather than begin with a description that his audience could not recognize, Paul referred to an altar bearing a dedication to an unknown god. He then developed an argument about the source of life, the limits of images made by human hands, and the relationship between divine reality and human seeking. The speech adopted elements of its setting while challenging some assumptions of that setting.","Responses were mixed. Some mocked his claims, some wanted to hear more, and others believed. The text therefore offers no easy picture of universal persuasion. For advanced readers, the important question is how a speaker builds a bridge without surrendering the substance of an argument. Paul's choice of an existing inscription is a rhetorical strategy: it starts from shared evidence, reinterprets its meaning, and invites the audience to reconsider what they suppose they already know."],"source":"Acts 17:16–34; original classroom rhetorical paraphrase (KJV)","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best captures the central teaching of this scripture-based reading?","choices":["Effective argument may begin with shared evidence and then challenge assumptions","Courage can draw on practiced skill rather than appearance","Compassion is demonstrated by helping a person in need","Wisdom includes recognizing both meaningful action and human limits"]},{"question":"Which person or group is most central to the account?","choices":["the Samaritan who helped the traveler","Esther, the queen","Paul speaking to listeners in Athens","David, the young shepherd"]},{"question":"What context or event establishes the situation?","choices":["a city filled with public debate and religious images","the road from Jerusalem to Jericho","a sequence of contrasting seasons in human life","Mordecai warns Esther about a threat to her people"]},{"question":"Which tension or problem drives the account?","choices":["people cannot command every outcome through effort","Naomi has lost her husband and sons","approaching the king uninvited is dangerous","the audience does not share every premise of Paul’s belief"]},{"question":"Which important action or decision is described?","choices":["David selects a sling and smooth stones","Paul refers to an altar dedicated to an unknown god","Esther prepares and arranges a meal to reveal the plot","the speaker sets paired experiences beside one another"]},{"question":"What result or response follows?","choices":["the threatened community gains protection","the reflection encourages humility and appreciation","the innkeeper receives money to continue care","listeners react with mockery, curiosity, or belief"]},{"question":"Which detail gives specific support to the account?","choices":["two earlier travelers pass without helping","Naomi releases her daughters-in-law from obligation","an existing inscription becomes a point of contact","David had defended sheep from dangerous animals"]},{"question":"What inference about motive or meaning is most justified?","choices":["persuasion requires attention to an audience without promising agreement","being a neighbor is shown through actions rather than labels","describing life’s changes is not endorsing every possible act","Ruth values belonging and faithfulness more than a promised reward"]},{"question":"Which phrase best explains a significant word or idea in the passage?","choices":["deliberate means planned with careful thought","commitment means a lasting promise shown in conduct","rhetorical means concerned with persuasive communication","mercy means compassionate help toward someone suffering"]},{"question":"Which broader interpretation is most consistent with the passage?","choices":["the apparent opposites create a nuanced rather than simple lesson","the mixed response shows the difference between a strong argument and universal acceptance","the narrative links influence to an obligation toward vulnerable people","the story respects grief while showing the strength of solidarity"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"12-5","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":5,"genre":"Novel","title":"First Impressions in Pride and Prejudice","paragraphs":["At a social gathering in rural England, Elizabeth Bennet encounters Mr. Darcy, a wealthy gentleman whose reserved manner seems cold and proud. He does little to correct the impression, and remarks attributed to him leave Elizabeth feeling insulted. Her lively intelligence helps her notice behavior that others overlook, but it does not protect her from every error. She begins constructing an account of Darcy's character from limited encounters and the stories of other people.","The social world around them rewards quick judgments. Marriage prospects, family reputation, and money shape conversation, often before anyone knows the full circumstances of another person's choices. As the novel develops, Elizabeth receives new information that complicates what she believed. Darcy, too, must confront the limitations of his own attitudes.","Austen's title points to tendencies that can operate together rather than in separate people. Pride may prevent honest communication; prejudice may make correction unwelcome. The narrative invites readers to hold judgments provisionally and examine how class expectations affect what characters see. Its central movement is not simply from dislike to affection but from certainty toward a more demanding understanding of others."],"source":"Austen, J. Pride and Prejudice. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1342.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1342","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["two future populations reveal the limits of the traveler’s first impression","a detective reconstructs an unexpected path from clues","a scientist proposes a machine that crosses time","a difficult acquaintance exposes the risk of hasty judgment"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["a human narrator and curious onlookers","Elizabeth Bennet and Mr. Darcy","Sherlock Holmes, Dr. Watson, and a frightened young woman","Sherlock Holmes and an unsuspecting shopkeeper"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a common in England where a strange cylinder lands","social gatherings in rural England","the social world of the Bennet family","a London shop and a nearby bank"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["early impressions are shaped by pride, gossip, and limited encounters","a valuable blue jewel is connected to an unlikely Christmas goose","observers cannot know whether the object is harmless or hostile","an easy, well-paid position seems too strange to be genuine"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["Elizabeth interprets Darcy’s behavior through her first dislike","the visitor observes the Eloi and searches for what their society conceals","Holmes inspects the room and tests explanations for its unusual details","the traveler investigates underground conditions and revises his theory"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the danger is connected to deliberate actions within the household","later information begins to challenge her certainty","curiosity turns to danger as the visitors reveal overwhelming power","her understanding of Darcy and herself becomes more complex"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["Darcy’s reserved manner initially appears insulting","the Morlocks live below while the Eloi remain above","the invaders lack protection against Earth’s familiar microbes","a strange sound and fixed bedroom features become crucial clues"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["confidence in a judgment is not proof that the judgment is fair","an imaginative claim must still confront evidence and explanation","social systems must be examined from more than one viewpoint","the unfamiliar should not be judged through wishful assumptions"]},{"question":"In context, what does “prejudice” mean?","choices":["the condition of being open to harm","a judgment formed before sufficient evidence","suggesting that something bad may happen","a person’s account of what they saw or experienced"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["critical thinking asks who benefits from a peculiar arrangement","understanding people requires revising impressions","ordinary objects can hold evidence that changes a case","uncertainty requires caution even when a spectacle is fascinating"]}],"answers":["D","B","B","A","A","B","A","A","B","B"]},{"id":"12-6","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":6,"genre":"Short Story","title":"The Blue Carbuncle: A Chain of Clues","paragraphs":["A strange collection of objects reaches Sherlock Holmes during the holiday season: an old hat, a goose, and eventually a precious blue gemstone. At first these objects seem unrelated. The challenge is to explain how a stolen jewel could have traveled through such ordinary surroundings. Holmes does not treat the oddness as proof of magic. He assumes that a sequence of human decisions connects the details.","Working with Dr. Watson, Holmes examines the hat and traces the bird through the people who handled it. Each discovery narrows the possibilities, but the detective must distinguish a useful observation from a merely interesting one. An object may suggest a person's habits without establishing that person's guilt. The jewel's unexpected location becomes meaningful only when placed within a larger chain of events.","The mystery illustrates both the promise and the risk of deductive thinking. Holmes's success depends on attention, but attention alone is insufficient: conclusions must be tested against what further witnesses and circumstances reveal. The episode invites readers to notice how a detective story makes information arrive gradually, requiring the reader to revise earlier explanations without abandoning the search for a coherent account. A sound deduction must survive the test of additional evidence."],"source":"Doyle, A. C. The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes. Project Gutenberg, eBook 1661.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1661","questions":[{"question":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of this reading?","choices":["an extraordinary arrival unsettles a confident society","a suspicious job advertisement conceals another goal","a detective reconstructs an unexpected path from clues","a future society raises questions about progress and dependence"]},{"question":"Which person or subject is central to the account?","choices":["Sherlock Holmes and Dr. Watson","a human narrator and curious onlookers","the Time Traveller and the Eloi","Sherlock Holmes and an unsuspecting shopkeeper"]},{"question":"Where does the selection mainly take place?","choices":["a London shop and a nearby bank","winter streets and rooms of London","the surface and underground of a future Earth","a Victorian drawing room"]},{"question":"What is the main complication discussed in the text?","choices":["military technology cannot stop the stronger invaders","a woman fears the circumstances surrounding her sister’s death","an easy, well-paid position seems too strange to be genuine","a valuable blue jewel is connected to an unlikely Christmas goose"]},{"question":"Which response or action is described in the passage?","choices":["a crowd gathers as the metal cylinder begins to open","Holmes follows small clues about ownership and delivery","Elizabeth reconsiders earlier accusations as new facts emerge","Holmes questions the advertisement and examines the arrangement"]},{"question":"What important result does the passage report?","choices":["the supposed opportunity is linked to a hidden criminal plan","her understanding of Darcy and herself becomes more complex","the inquiry reveals a crime behind an ordinary household object","skepticism remains until further events challenge the visitors"]},{"question":"Which detail provides evidence for the reading?","choices":["a hat and a bird both become sources of evidence","the employer’s sudden disappearance makes the offer suspicious","the Morlocks live below while the Eloi remain above","the approaching Martians arrive in an engineered object rather than a natural meteor"]},{"question":"What conclusion can be drawn from the information given?","choices":["the unfamiliar should not be judged through wishful assumptions","an imaginative claim must still confront evidence and explanation","sound inference requires connecting details without jumping too far","intellectual humility makes genuine growth possible"]},{"question":"In context, what does “deduction” mean?","choices":["reasoning from evidence toward a conclusion","the condition of being open to harm","a person’s account of what they saw or experienced","a judgment formed before sufficient evidence"]},{"question":"What broader idea is reinforced by the passage?","choices":["critical thinking asks who benefits from a peculiar arrangement","understanding people requires revising impressions","ordinary objects can hold evidence that changes a case","inequality can persist beneath an attractive public appearance"]}],"answers":["C","A","B","D","B","C","A","C","A","C"]},{"id":"12-7","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":7,"genre":"Essay","title":"Independent Thought and the Burden of Proof","paragraphs":["An independent thinker is not merely someone who disagrees. Disagreement can become a habit as unexamined as agreement. To judge for oneself requires an effort to understand a claim, identify its assumptions, and weigh the evidence offered in its support. Ralph Waldo Emerson's writing about self-reliance gives literary force to intellectual independence, yet modern readers must still ask what independence demands when knowledge is specialized and collective.","Consider a student evaluating a report on climate. Rejecting it simply because it comes from an institution is no more rigorous than accepting it because the institution is famous. Responsible judgment asks which observations were collected, how uncertainty was reported, whether other evidence agrees, and what competing explanations remain. Trust is not the opposite of thought; uncritical trust is.","The burden of proof also depends on the claim. A modest conclusion may require modest evidence, while an extraordinary assertion demands much stronger support. Intellectual autonomy therefore involves humility as well as confidence. It allows a person to revise an opinion when better evidence appears. The goal is not to become impossible to persuade, but to become difficult to mislead."],"source":"Original critical essay in dialogue with Emerson, Self-Reliance in Essays","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/16643","questions":[{"question":"Which statement is the essay’s primary claim?","choices":["Independent judgment needs evidence and willingness to revise","An experiment should distinguish a measured result from a universal claim","A trustworthy inference considers context and alternatives","Social pressure can make obvious errors seem acceptable"]},{"question":"Which example does the writer use to clarify the claim?","choices":["an object discovered near a crime","the gardener and student examples","a student evaluating a climate report","NASA’s DART collision with Dimorphos"]},{"question":"Which difficulty or mistaken belief is discussed?","choices":["confusing disagreement with independent reasoning","the expectation that results should arrive immediately","the limits of attention during a first reading","uncertainty about applying one test to every asteroid"]},{"question":"Which contrast is used to develop the argument?","choices":["waiting without action versus purposeful persistence","reading once for events versus twice for interpretation","dramatic guessing contrasted with evidence-based reasoning","uncritical acceptance versus automatic rejection"]},{"question":"Which supporting action or reasoning appears in the essay?","choices":["the investigator compares observations with new facts","the reader evaluates observations, uncertainty, and alternatives","a spacecraft strikes a moonlet to change orbital motion","the tortoise keeps moving toward the finish"]},{"question":"What consequence or outcome does the writer identify?","choices":["the result informs but does not settle planetary-defense questions","multiple consistent details strengthen a conclusion","an unsupported guess can become a checked explanation","a reasoned position remains open to better evidence"]},{"question":"Which detail most directly supports the argument?","choices":["students first track unfamiliar words and names","fictional detectives encounter evidence arranged by an author","a famous institution is not itself a complete proof","people repeat claims without checking sources"]},{"question":"What inference follows from the writer’s reasoning?","choices":["stronger claims ordinarily demand stronger supporting evidence","comprehension improves when readers verify a claim","habits can be controlled more directly than outcomes","one successful demonstration does not guarantee all future outcomes"]},{"question":"Which phrase most accurately explains a key term from the essay?","choices":["kinetic impact means changing motion through a collision","investigation means a careful search for answers and evidence","autonomy means self-directed responsible judgment","scrutiny means careful and critical examination"]},{"question":"Which conclusion best respects the essay’s qualifications?","choices":["the best interpretation separates narrative excitement from reliable proof","intellectual humility and critical independence can coexist","questioning a claim respectfully may protect a community from error","scientific confidence grows when conclusions include explicit limits"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"12-8","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":8,"genre":"News Report","title":"DART and the Test of Deflection","paragraphs":["In September 2022, NASA's Double Asteroid Redirection Test, known as DART, deliberately crashed a spacecraft into Dimorphos, a small moonlet orbiting the asteroid Didymos. The collision was not an attempt to prevent an imminent disaster. It was an experiment designed to test whether a kinetic impact could alter the movement of a small celestial body.","Scientists observed the system before and after the collision. They found that the impact changed Dimorphos's orbital period and affected its physical shape. The result offered evidence that a spacecraft could modify an asteroid's path under the right conditions. Yet one successful experiment does not mean every threatening object could be handled in exactly the same way. Size, composition, available warning time, and orbit would matter.","DART is useful to readers as a model of scientific reasoning. Researchers identified a practical question, designed a measurable test, gathered observations, and examined the limits of the conclusion. The story is a news event, but its lasting significance lies in the disciplined process by which an idea became supported by evidence. The test supplied evidence about deflection by intentional impact."],"source":"NASA. Asteroid Facts.","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/","questions":[{"question":"Which description best captures the selection as a whole?","choices":["sailors seek the truth about a supposed sea monster","the character’s actions challenge his stated weakness","an intentional spacecraft impact tests a defense idea","systematic observation supports asteroid-risk decisions"]},{"question":"Who or what is at the center of this selection?","choices":["Captain Nemo and Professor Aronnax","planetary-defense scientists","asteroids and the scientists who study them","NASA’s DART mission and an asteroid moonlet"]},{"question":"Which setting or context is most important here?","choices":["observatories on Earth and in space","space near the asteroid system Didymos","the near and far sides of the Moon","a submerged landscape"]},{"question":"Which problem or tension drives the passage?","choices":["walking beneath the sea seems impossible without specialized equipment","scientists need evidence that a spacecraft could alter an asteroid’s movement","small rocky bodies are often mistaken for identical debris","the Scarecrow judges himself by what he lacks rather than by what he does"]},{"question":"How does the principal character or subject address the problem?","choices":["DART deliberately strikes the moonlet Dimorphos in September 2022","Aronnax joins a voyage to investigate the reports","his companions notice his useful suggestions during difficulties","scientists locate, track, and refine the predicted paths of objects"]},{"question":"Which outcome is described?","choices":["the quest exposes a gap between self-image and demonstrated ability","asteroids reveal information about the solar system’s formation","measurements show that the impact changed the moonlet’s orbit","the supposed sea monster is revealed as the submarine Nautilus"]},{"question":"Which supporting detail is stated in the selection?","choices":["Nemo’s independence depends on living apart from ordinary society","the mission tested deflection rather than responding to a known immediate threat","both lunar hemispheres receive sunlight at different times","NASA coordinates planetary-defense observations and warnings"]},{"question":"Which inference is most reasonable from the passage?","choices":["classification can reveal a history concealed by appearance","extraordinary claims should be tested against better evidence","a new observation can force the revision of an earlier conclusion","controlled experiments can inform future safety strategies"]},{"question":"In context, what does “deflection” mean?","choices":["the way a person thinks about their own character","completely surrounded or covered by liquid","happening at the same rate or in matching timing","a change in the direction or course of movement"]},{"question":"Which theme or broader lesson is best supported?","choices":["preparation is more reliable when methods are tested beforehand","credible warnings depend on repeated evidence rather than fear","small objects can preserve important evidence about the past","competence is better assessed by evidence than by a label"]}],"answers":["C","D","B","B","A","C","B","D","D","A"]},{"id":"12-9","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":9,"genre":"Poetry","title":"An Atlas of Unanswered Questions","paragraphs":["On the atlas each boundary looks resolved,\na thin authority of ink against the page.\nBeyond the margin, however, coastlines move,\nand rivers alter their negotiated age.","A name can steady what the eye observes;\nit cannot make a changing world stand still.\nThe chart has scales, conventions, measured curves,\nand empty spaces drawn by human will.","I thought a map would cancel every doubt,\nas if a legend guaranteed the ground.\nInstead, its careful symbols pointed out\nhow much depended on where north was found.","A satellite can register a line;\na traveler knows the road beneath the rain.\nBetween their accounts, no single sign\ncontains the full particularity of terrain.","So let the atlas open, not conclude;\nlet every border prompt another look.\nThe most exact description still is viewed\nthrough questions no coordinate can book."],"source":"Original classroom poem about models, representation, and limits of knowledge; factual context NOAA weather and climate","sourceUrl":"https://www.noaa.gov/explainers/what-s-difference-between-climate-and-weather","questions":[{"question":"What is the central idea expressed by the poem?","choices":["Representations are useful but cannot contain reality completely","Small, steady progress offers a lesson for living","Knowledge grows through questioning rather than mere possession of books","A storm can change how a person perceives ordinary life"]},{"question":"Which image is central to its meaning?","choices":["a creek moving beside a gate","a changing sea and its shoreline","an atlas with boundaries and symbols","a lantern between library shelves"]},{"question":"What is the main setting or situation in the poem?","choices":["a map considered against a changing physical world","a dim room looking toward a street at dusk","a coast seen through night and dawn","a library during an evening reading session"]},{"question":"Which change is experienced or observed by the speaker?","choices":["the speaker moves from busyness to reflection","a leaf moves while a pebble stays still","the speaker moves from certainty toward inquiry","the speaker abandons the hope that maps eliminate uncertainty"]},{"question":"Which contrast or comparison shapes the imagery?","choices":["the speaker takes steps beside the water","satellite measurement contrasts with lived experience","the moon and local wind are contrasted","rain and quiet become connected images"]},{"question":"What emotional or intellectual movement does the poem show?","choices":["measurement opens rather than closes interpretation","the speaker accepts a more careful explanation","the creek continues after the speaker goes home","the reader gains a more qualified trust in models"]},{"question":"Which textual detail most clearly supports an interpretation?","choices":["the water keeps finding a way through bends","the speaker writes a figure and also a question","boundaries look fixed although land and rivers change","waves and tide provide evidence of ongoing change"]},{"question":"What might the central image or situation suggest?","choices":["different perspectives reveal different kinds of information","distant astronomy reveals limits of immediate perception","a text is useful when the reader examines it independently","the stream suggests patient persistence"]},{"question":"Which option best explains one important word or expression?","choices":["steady means continuing without sudden stops","sufficient means enough to explain or satisfy a need","convention means an agreed method of representing something","inquiry means purposeful questioning or investigation"]},{"question":"How should the closing lines be understood?","choices":["the final color image suggests changed perception rather than magic","the poem supports precise maps while questioning claims of total certainty","the ending suggests that learning can continue beyond one walk","the final stanza treats uncertainty as intellectual opportunity"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]},{"id":"12-10","grade":12,"color":"Diamond","num":10,"genre":"Science & Nonfiction","title":"Planetary Defense as Evidence and Ethics","paragraphs":["NASA's DART mission demonstrated that a spacecraft could change the motion of an asteroid moonlet by striking it. That result supports the possibility of using a kinetic impactor in planetary defense, but it does not remove the need for difficult decisions. A hazardous object's size, internal structure, orbit, and the time available before a possible encounter would affect any proposed response.","Researchers would also need to consider how confidently the object's future trajectory was known. A small uncertainty across decades may be manageable, while a poorly understood path could make an intervention hard to evaluate. A collision intended to reduce risk should be studied for possible unintended outcomes. Scientific capability and responsible authorization are therefore separate questions.","The ethical dimension extends beyond engineering. Who should participate in a decision that might affect different regions of Earth? How should experts communicate uncertainty without causing panic or offering false reassurance? An advanced reader should resist both extremes: treating one successful experiment as a universal solution, or dismissing useful evidence because some uncertainty remains. Evidence-based planning requires technical competence, international discussion, and a clear account of consequences."],"source":"Original science-and-ethics discussion grounded in NASA DART mission overview","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dart/","questions":[{"question":"Which statement summarizes the principal scientific idea?","choices":["planetary defense needs scientific evidence and responsible decisions","models simplify systems while preserving important relationships","weather describes short-term conditions while climate describes longer patterns","the Moon’s motion explains a common error in language"]},{"question":"Which phenomenon or evidence is discussed?","choices":["temperature, pressure, wind, and moisture data","asteroids and the scientists who study them","the DART collision with an asteroid moonlet","wind, rain, temperature, and clouds"]},{"question":"Which observations or methods are emphasized?","choices":["trajectory, composition, size, and available response time","mathematical relationships and observed starting conditions","orbits within and beyond the main asteroid belt","Earth’s path through space"]},{"question":"What factor or complication is central to the explanation?","choices":["the popular phrase dark side of the Moon gives a false impression","day and year must be distinguished despite their familiar names","small rocky bodies are often mistaken for identical debris","possible uncertainty and unintended consequences"]},{"question":"Which process or contrast does the text explain?","choices":["models and direct observations serve different roles","mission data inform but do not settle every future decision","researchers compare compositions, paths, and impact records","the essay distinguishes a far side from a permanently unlit side"]},{"question":"What consequence or implication is described?","choices":["records allow researchers to describe long-term distributions","asteroids reveal information about the solar system’s formation","readers can explain the calendar’s occasional extra day","risk planning requires engineering and public deliberation"]},{"question":"Which observation gives evidence for the explanation?","choices":["Earth takes about 365.25 days to complete one orbit","one afternoon cannot establish a multidecade trend","different regions may be affected by a shared decision","many asteroids orbit between Mars and Jupiter"]},{"question":"What interpretation of the evidence is supported?","choices":["technical success does not replace ethical authorization","human calendars are models adjusted to natural cycles","classification can reveal a history concealed by appearance","familiar expressions can mislead when taken literally"]},{"question":"Which option best explains a key technical term?","choices":["projection means a possible future estimated under assumptions","infallible means incapable of making mistakes","trajectory means the path followed by a moving body","the materials from which something is made"]},{"question":"Which limitation or qualification does the text stress?","choices":["accurate measurement sometimes requires practical adjustment","responsible choices require both workable methods and transparent uncertainty","scientific terms should correct rather than reinforce misconceptions","small objects can preserve important evidence about the past"]}],"answers":["A","C","A","D","B","D","C","A","C","B"]}];
